# 1. Application Introduction
 
TrackMyWealth is a privacy-first personal finance, portfolio and wealth-management application for private individuals and households in Switzerland and Germany. Its purpose is to bring financial data that is normally fragmented across banks, brokers, pension providers, credit-card providers and other financial institutions into one coherent, user-controlled system.
 
The application combines household cash-flow and budgeting functions with advanced private-investor portfolio analytics. A user shall be able to understand not only how an investment portfolio performs, but also how salary, spending, saving, investing, liabilities and long-term wealth interact.
 
**Core product promise:** one private application that shows where money is held, how it flows, how investments perform, what assets and liabilities exist, and the resulting consolidated net worth.
 
## 1.1 Market context and competitive positioning
 
Four products define the competitive field. None occupies the position TrackMyWealth targets.
 
| Product | Position | Strength | Gap relative to TrackMyWealth |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Parqet** (DE, ~350k users) | DACH portfolio tracker | Deepest German broker ingestion; PDF parsing for 50+ brokers; broker-reported holdings reconciliation; ETF look-through; FIFO cost basis | Assets only, no budgeting; German-centric; no Swiss pension; cloud-only; paid tiers gate the valuable analytics |
| **getquin** (DE, ~500k users) | Wealth platform with social layer | Widest European account connectivity; AI document import; instrument-level community | Retrofitting cashflow onto an investment model — had to split cash into a separate account type in mid-2026, relocating user balances; reported accuracy problems with corporate actions and transfers |
| **Ghostfolio** (CH, open source) | Self-hosted wealth tracker | Data sovereignty; documented REST API; clean six-type activity model; deliberate scope restraint | No broker connectivity at all; no budgeting; no pension; AGPL constrains commercial reuse |
| **BlueBudget** (CH) | Swiss budgeting app | Swiss open banking via SIX bLink; budgets derived from real transactions; expense splitting; strong behavioural design | No securities capability; Switzerland only; young product |
 
**Two structural gaps follow:**
 
- **Nobody covers both sides of the balance sheet in one coherent model.** Trackers ignore spending; budgeting apps ignore investments. The one product attempting both is visibly paying the cost of retrofitting.
 
- **Nobody serves Switzerland and Germany equally.** The German leader has no Swiss pension competence; the Swiss leader has no securities capability. Cross-border households — CH residents with German brokers, DE residents with Swiss accounts, cross-border commuters — currently run two tools and reconcile by hand.
 
## 1.2 Differentiation pillars
 
Each pillar is an architecture commitment, not a feature to be added later.
 
| # | Pillar | Why it is defensible |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D1 | **Unified balance-sheet and cashflow domain model**, designed as one from day one | Retrofitting is the documented failure of the nearest competitor; the cost of doing it later is user-visible data migration |
| D2 | **Pension folded into the whole picture** — Pillar 3a holdings counted in consolidated asset allocation and concentration analysis | No analysed product does this. For a Swiss user with a large 3a equity allocation, any concentration analysis that omits it is simply wrong. |
| D3 | **Dual-jurisdiction CH/DE households treated as normal**, with a full multi-tier currency model | The market is structurally split; nobody serves the overlap |
| D4 | **Measurement quality** — TWR and MWR side by side, currency attribution, reconciliation against provider-reported balances, standards-based classification | Trackers lose users to wrong numbers, not missing features |
| D5 | **Deployment sovereignty** — the household may run the entire system on infrastructure it controls, with direct access to its own database | Only Ghostfolio offers self-hosting, and it has no cashflow, budgeting or pension capability. Parqet and getquin are cloud-only. |
 
**Positioning caution.** D1–D5 are architecture-led and therefore slow for a competitor to copy — but also slow to demonstrate on a landing page. A feature such as a tax export would have been easier to show and is out of scope. Launch messaging must solve this deliberately (see OPEN-018).
 
# 2. Product Vision and Objectives
 
TrackMyWealth shall provide a single financial system of record capable of answering:
 
- What do I or my household own?
 
- What do I or my household owe?
 
- Where is the money held?
 
- How much cash is available?
 
- How much was earned, spent, saved and invested?
 
- How are investments performing?
 
- What are Time-Weighted Return (TWR) and Money-Weighted Return (MWR)?
 
- How are assets allocated by asset class, GICS sector/industry, currency, country, institution and account?
 
- How has net worth developed over time?
 
- How are financial goals progressing?
 
- **Does what the application shows agree with what the bank or broker reports?**
 
The application is an information, consolidation, analysis and reporting system. It shall not execute payments, bank transfers or security trades.
 
# 3. Scope
 
## 3.1 Geographic scope
 
- Switzerland (CH)
 
- Germany (DE)
 
Country-specific functionality, terminology, institutions and integrations shall focus on these two markets. The domain model shall avoid hard-coding that would prevent future expansion. Austria is the natural third market; connectivity built for Germany is largely reusable there, jurisdiction-specific logic is not.
 
## 3.2 Language scope
 
- English
 
- German
 
**Note for planning:** DE+EN covers Germany fully and German-speaking Switzerland, roughly two-thirds of the Swiss market. The Romandie and Ticino are not served. French should be treated as the first post-launch language, and no design decision may foreclose FR/IT.
 
## 3.3 Financial scope
 
- Bank/current accounts and savings accounts
 
- Securities accounts / depots
 
- Pension accounts, including Swiss Pillar 3a and Swiss Pillar 2 / vested-benefit accounts
 
- Discretionary / managed mandates (robo-advisors and managed 3a products)
 
- Credit cards
 
- Mortgages and loans
 
- Cryptocurrency accounts/wallets
 
- Cash, securities, pensions, real estate, commodities, precious metals and custom assets
 
- Household cash flow, budgeting, savings and financial goals
 
- Portfolio valuation, asset allocation, TWR, MWR, dividends and benchmark comparison
 
# 4. Explicitly Out of Scope
 
| ID | Capability | Decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OOS-001 | Brokerage / trade execution | Not supported |
| OOS-002 | Payment execution | Not supported |
| OOS-003 | Professional financial advice | Not supported |
| OOS-004 | Tax calculations, tax reporting, tax-return preparation or filing | Not supported at all |
| OOS-005 | Private-equity valuation | Not supported |
| OOS-006 | Public social network / public portfolio feed | Not supported |
| OOS-007 | Stock screening and investment research terminal | Not supported. Ghostfolio omits this entirely and remains viable; large scope, low differentiation. |
| OOS-008 | **User-facing data export** | **Not a requirement at present.** Deferred to post-MVP (section 38). See section 31 for the residual obligation that cannot be deferred. |
 
## 4.1 Tax exclusion — retained residue
 
Tax functionality is out of scope. However, **tax amounts are a cash event that changes what the user actually received**, and cannot be discarded without making returns wrong. The following is retained explicitly as *return mechanics*, not as tax functionality:
 
| ID | Retained | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| FR-TAXR-001 [MUST] | Tax deducted at source is stored as a distinct amount on the transaction, and gross versus net income is distinguishable | A gross dividend of 100 with 35% withheld puts 65 in the user’s account. Recording only the net figure makes dividend analysis wrong; recording only the gross figure makes the cash balance wrong. |
| FR-TAXR-002 [MUST] | Withholding-source country is retained on the security master | Used to explain a reduced dividend receipt. No reclaim logic is built. |
| FR-TAXR-003 [MUST] | Cost-basis method remains a configurable accounting parameter | Required for realised gain/loss and for reconciliation against broker statements. Accounting requirement, not tax. |
| FR-TAXR-004 [MUST] | Tax-related source fields are never dropped at import | Historical withholding data cannot be reconstructed later. Capturing it costs nothing now and is impossible to backfill. |
 
**Design instruction:** do not remove tax fields from the schema. Should tax reporting ever be reinstated, the historical data must already be present.
 
# 5. Target Users
 
| User group | Primary needs |
| --- | --- |
| Beginner / private investor | Simple overview, understandable returns, easy imports, budgeting, savings, net worth, goals |
| Advanced private investor | Consolidated depots, master data, prices, TWR/MWR, benchmarks, GICS, asset/currency/country exposure |
| Family / household | Multiple users and owners, shared/private accounts, mortgages, credit cards, household budget, consolidated wealth |
| **Cross-border household** | CH resident with a German broker, DE resident with Swiss accounts, or a cross-border commuter earning CHF and spending EUR. Needs dual-currency consolidation and both markets’ institutions in one place. Currently forced to run two tools. |
| **Privacy-motivated user** | Rejects cloud aggregation of financial data. Needs the ability to self-host with no dependency on the project. This persona is the reason for the self-hosted topology and must not be traded away for convenience features. |
| **Financial-independence planner** | Models time to independence: savings rate, withdrawal scenarios, net-worth trajectory |
| System administrator | Manage users, roles, account status and system access |
 
# 6. Product Principles
 
**PR-001 — Privacy-first [MUST]**: Financial data shall be protected as a primary product requirement.
 
**PR-002 — Deployment sovereignty [MUST]**: A user or household shall be able to run the entire system on infrastructure they control, with no dependency on a service operated by the project. The same application, API and schema serve both topologies; there is no reduced self-hosted variant and no separate codebase.
 
*This supersedes the earlier local-first formulation. The client is an online API consumer in both topologies (section 48). Sovereignty is achieved by letting the user own the server, not by removing the server.*
 
**PR-003 — User ownership [MUST]**: A household’s data belongs to that household. In the self-hosted topology this is achieved directly: the household controls the database. In the hosted topology it is achieved through backup, restore and the statutory portability obligation of section 31.
 
*Amended: user-facing export is no longer a product requirement (OOS-008). The ownership principle is retained because it drives the deployment model and the data-protection obligations, not because of the export feature.*
 
**PR-004 — Unified financial model [MUST]**: Budgeting and investing shall share one coherent domain model.
 
**PR-005 — Financial correctness [MUST]**: Calculations shall be deterministic, reproducible and testable.
 
**PR-006 — Progressive complexity [MUST]**: Beginners receive clear summaries; advanced users can drill into professional analytics.
 
**PR-007 — Read/analyze only [MUST]**: The system does not move money or execute trades.
 
**PR-008 — Extensible architecture [MUST]**: Institutions, data providers and account types shall be connected through abstractions.
 
**PR-009 — Transparent classification [MUST]**: Source standards and user/reporting classifications shall be stored separately.
 
**PR-010 — Correctness is the product [MUST]**: Where correctness and feature scope conflict, correctness wins. Competing trackers lose user trust through wrong numbers — mishandled corporate actions, missing cost basis, double-counted transfers — not through missing features. A wrong number displayed confidently is worse than a figure withheld with an explanation.
 
**PR-011 — No silent estimates [MUST]**: Any value that is estimated, interpolated, carried forward or derived from incomplete data shall be visibly marked as such. The system shall never present an approximation with the same visual authority as a reconciled figure.
 
**PR-012 — Graceful degradation [MUST]**: Failure of any external dependency — price provider, FX source, institution connector, classification data — shall degrade the affected feature only. It shall never block access to data already stored.
 
# 7. High-Level Financial Domain
 
TrackMyWealth
|
+-- System Administration
|   +-- Users
|   +-- Roles / Permissions
|
+-- Household / Financial Context
    +-- Members / Owners
    +-- Financial Institutions (Containers)
        +-- Accounts
            +-- Transactions
            +-- Holdings / Positions
            +-- Balances
            +-- Snapshots (provider-reported)
            +-- Valuations
    +-- Security Master
    +-- Budgets
    +-- Goals
    +-- Consolidated Reporting
 
A Financial Institution is a container. It is not itself a bank account. Examples include PostFinance, Yuh, DKB, Sparkasse and VIAC. One container may hold different account types simultaneously.
 
# 8. User Administration and Household Model
 
## 8.1 System user versus household member
 
A System User is a person who can authenticate to TrackMyWealth. A Household Member is a person represented in the financial model. These concepts shall be separate; a child or dependent may be represented financially without having a login.
 
**FR-USR-001 — User administration [MUST]**: The system shall provide user administration for authorized administrators.
 
**FR-USR-002 — Initial administrator [MUST]**: Initial setup shall establish at least one System Administrator.
 
**FR-USR-003 — Create/edit/disable users [MUST]**: Administrators shall create, edit, disable and reactivate users.
 
**FR-USR-004 — Roles [MUST]**: The initial roles shall include System Administrator and Standard User; the model shall support future expansion.
 
**FR-USR-005 — Last-admin protection [MUST]**: The last active administrator shall not be disabled, deleted or demoted without another active administrator.
 
**FR-USR-006 — User language [MUST]**: Each user shall have an English or German language preference.
 
**FR-USR-007 — User reporting currency [MUST]**: A reporting currency shall be selected for each user profile and used for that user’s consolidated views.
 
**FR-USR-008 — Household link [MUST]**: A System User may be linked to a Household Member.
 
**FR-USR-009 — RBAC [MUST]**: Administrative permissions shall be enforced through role-based authorization, not UI hiding alone.
 
**FR-USR-010 — Financial-access separation [MUST]**: System administration rights and financial-data access shall be modeled as distinct permission concerns.
 
**FR-USR-011 — Disabled user data retention [MUST]**: Disabling a System User shall not delete or orphan the financial data of the linked Household Member. Ownership records reference the Member, not the User.
 
## 8.2 Ownership model
 
- Individual ownership
 
- Joint ownership
 
- Household ownership
 
- Child/dependent
 
**FR-HOU-001 — Household [MUST]**: The financial model shall support one or more household members/owners.
 
**FR-HOU-002 — Ownership assignment [MUST]**: Accounts and relevant assets/liabilities shall be assignable to an owner or ownership context.
 
**FR-HOU-003 — Ownership percentage [SHOULD]**: Fractional ownership should be supported where needed.
 
**FR-HOU-004 — Future privacy levels [SHOULD]**: The architecture should support no access, balance-only, read, edit and full access for future multi-user sharing.
 
**FR-HOU-005 — Ownership-aware aggregation [MUST]**: Where fractional or joint ownership applies, consolidated figures scoped to a single person shall apply the ownership share. Household-scoped figures shall count the full amount exactly once, never double-counting a jointly owned account across two members.
 
**FR-HOU-006 — Ownership changes are dated [SHOULD]**: A change of ownership shall carry an effective date so historical reports remain reproducible.
 
# 9. Financial Institution / Container
 
The Financial Institution (also referred to as Institution or Container) is the grouping level for products provided by one bank, broker, pension provider or similar financial provider.
 
**FR-INS-001 — Institution container [MUST]**: The system shall model institutions independently from accounts.
 
**FR-INS-002 — Multiple accounts [MUST]**: A container shall support zero to many accounts of different types.
 
**FR-INS-003 — Custom institutions [MUST]**: Users shall be able to add institutions not predefined by TrackMyWealth.
 
**FR-INS-004 — Container currency [MUST]**: When a container is created, a container reporting currency shall be selected.
 
**FR-INS-005 — No currency restriction [MUST]**: The container currency shall not restrict the currencies of accounts or securities inside the container.
 
**FR-INS-006 — Institution summary [MUST]**: Every container shall have a dedicated Institution Summary View.
 
**FR-INS-007 — Institution catalogue [MUST]**: A curated catalogue of Swiss and German institutions shall ship as seed data — name, country, type, identifier, logo, available import methods — searchable at container creation. The catalogue shall be maintained as configuration data and updatable without an application release.
 
**FR-INS-008 — Institution type is descriptive only [MUST]**: An institution’s type (bank, broker, pension provider, card issuer, asset manager, exchange, other) is metadata for display and filtering. It shall never constrain which account types may be created beneath it. PostFinance is a bank, a broker and a 3a provider simultaneously; Yuh is a bank and a broker. Any validation rule coupling institution type to account type will break the first time a provider expands its offering, and will break for existing users.
 
**FR-INS-009 — Institution reassignment [SHOULD]**: Accounts shall be movable between containers without data loss. Institutions merge, rebrand and are acquired.
 
**FR-INS-010 — Missing connector never blocks [MUST]**: Any institution may be created and fully used with manual or file-based data alone. Automated connectivity is an optional enhancement layered on top, never a precondition.
 
## 9.1 Institution Summary View
 
Where applicable, the summary shall aggregate:
 
- Total assets, total liabilities and net value
 
- Cash balances and savings
 
- Investment/depot value
 
- Pension value
 
- Mortgages, loans and credit-card liabilities
 
- Income, expenses, savings and cash flow
 
- Account overview
 
- Investment performance, TWR, MWR and asset allocation for investment accounts
 
**FR-INS-SUM-001 — Container summary currency [MUST]**: Aggregated values in the Institution Summary shall be displayed in the container currency.
 
**FR-INS-SUM-002 — Drill-down [MUST]**: Institution summary values shall drill down to contributing accounts, positions or transactions.
 
**FR-INS-SUM-003 — Mixed asset and liability display [MUST]**: Where a container holds both assets and liabilities, the summary net value may legitimately be negative — a Sparkasse container holding a current account and a mortgage is the normal case. Negative container totals shall be displayed correctly and shall not be presented as an error state.
 
**FR-INS-SUM-004 — Summary currency transparency [MUST]**: Where the summary converts from account currencies to the container currency, the applied FX rate and its date shall be inspectable.
 
## 9.2 Container rules
 
The container is a generic grouping device. It holds no balance, no behaviour and no currency of its own beyond its chosen reporting currency.
 
| Rule | Statement |
| --- | --- |
| C1 | A container holds 0..n accounts of **heterogeneous types** in any mixture |
| C2 | An account belongs to **exactly one** container at a time (reassignable per FR-INS-009) |
| C3 | **Container type never constrains account type** (FR-INS-008) |
| C4 | The **same account type may occur repeatedly** in one container — three current accounts at UBS, two Pillar 3a accounts at VIAC, which is normal Swiss practice for staggered withdrawal. Account identity is therefore never the pair (container, type). |
| C5 | A container may hold accounts in **different currencies** simultaneously |
| C6 | A container may mix assets and liabilities, so its roll-up may be **negative** (FR-INS-SUM-003) |
| C7 | A container may be **empty** and remain valid |
| C8 | All container-level figures are **derived**, never stored |
| C9 | **A container need not be an institution.** A default *Personal Assets* container holds items held with no provider — real estate, vehicles, precious metals, collectibles. Every account has a parent; some parents are not companies. |
 
**FR-INS-011 — Personal Assets container [MUST]**: A default container shall exist for assets and liabilities not held at any institution, so that the tree remains uniform and no account is orphaned. See OPEN-012 regarding whether a privately held mortgage attaches here or to the lending institution.
 
**FR-INS-012 — Extensibility verification [MUST]**: Introducing a new account type shall require no change to container logic, tree navigation or the consolidation layer. *Verification: add a new account type in a test build and confirm no consolidation code is modified.*
 
# 10. Account Model
 
| Account type | Purpose / examples |
| --- | --- |
| Cash / Current Account | Privatkonto, Girokonto, multi-currency cash account |
| Savings Account | Sparkonto, Tagesgeld |
| Securities Account / Depot | Stocks, ETFs, funds, bonds and other securities |
| Managed Mandate | Discretionary portfolio managed by the provider (robo-advisor, managed 3a) |
| Pension Account | Generic retirement/pension account; Swiss Pillar 3a as regional classification |
| Vested Benefits / Occupational Pension | Swiss Pillar 2 / Freizügigkeit, German bAV |
| Credit Card | Individual purchases plus outstanding liability |
| Mortgage | Liability account |
| Loan | Liability account |
| Cryptocurrency Account / Wallet | Exchange, hardware/software wallet or manual wallet |
| Custom Asset | Real estate, vehicles, precious metals, collectibles |
 
**FR-ACC-001 — Account attributes [MUST]**: Each account shall store institution, name, account type, owner, native currency, status and optional identifiers.
 
**FR-ACC-002 — Native account currency [MUST]**: Each account shall retain its own account currency independently of container and user reporting currencies.
 
**FR-ACC-003 — Archive accounts [MUST]**: Closed accounts shall be archivable without deleting history.
 
**FR-ACC-004 — Pillar 3a [MUST]**: Swiss Pillar 3a shall be represented as a Swiss classification of a generic pension account.
 
**FR-ACC-005 — Account type is immutable [MUST]**: An account’s type shall not change after creation. A depot does not become a mortgage. Where conversion is genuinely required, it is close-and-recreate with an explicit migration, never an in-place update.
 
**FR-ACC-006 — Archived accounts remain in history [MUST]**: An archived account shall be excluded from current totals but included in all historical series and reports covering periods when it was active.
 
## 10.1 Account capability model
 
The account **type** answers *what it is*. **Capabilities** answer *what it can do* — and capabilities are where behaviour actually lives.
 
**FR-ACC-010 — Declared capabilities [MUST]**: Each account shall carry declared, queryable capability flags rather than having behaviour inferred from its type in application logic. Minimum set: holds_positions, has_transactions, has_statement_cycle, has_amortisation, has_contribution_limit, is_discretionary, manual_valuation.
 
**FR-ACC-011 — Capability, not sub-type, drives variation [MUST]**: Behaviour that varies within a type shall be expressed as capability data, not as additional sub-types. **Pension is the proof case:** a VIAC Pillar 3a holds fund positions and must be performance-measurable exactly like a depot; a PostFinance Pillar 3a may be interest-bearing only; a Pillar 2 is a vested benefit with no visible positions. Creating SecuritiesPension, CashPension and OccupationalPension as separate types multiplies with every provider and jurisdiction added. One pension type with capability flags does not.
 
**FR-ACC-012 — Uniform valuation interface [MUST]**: Every account type, whatever its internals, shall expose the same contract to the rest of the system: a signed value in its own currency at any given date, a currency, an asset/liability nature and a parent container. Consolidation, net worth, allocation and navigation shall consume only this interface and shall never branch on account type.
 
**FR-ACC-013 — Nature derived from type [MUST]**: Asset/liability nature shall be derived from account type and applied as a sign in all aggregation, so that a mortgage can never be added to rather than subtracted from net worth.
 
## 10.2 Discretionary / managed mandates
 
Relevant to Swiss providers such as VIAC, frankly, True Wealth and Selma, and to managed depots generally.
 
**FR-ACC-020 — Snapshot-led maintenance [SHOULD]**: For accounts flagged is_discretionary, the provider rebalances without user action. Holdings shall be maintained primarily from provider-reported snapshots rather than reconstructed from user-entered trades. The system shall not treat unexplained holding changes as missing data, and shall not prompt the user to add a missing trade.
 
**FR-ACC-021 — Management fees recorded [SHOULD]**: Periodic management fees shall be recorded as fee transactions so that they appear in fee analysis and reduce measured return. Tracking a mandate independently of the provider’s own reporting is only worthwhile if the fees are visible in the result.
 
**FR-ACC-022 — Default performance metric [SHOULD]**: TWR shall be the default headline metric for discretionary accounts and MWR for self-directed accounts, because TWR isolates the manager’s contribution from the user’s contribution timing. The user may override the default.
 
## 10.3 Occupational pension and vested benefits
 
**FR-ACC-030 — Balance-and-entitlement model [SHOULD]**: Swiss Pillar 2 and German bAV accounts shall be modelled as balance-only accounts updated from the annual pension certificate — vested benefit, interest credit, employer and employee contributions. They have no visible underlying positions, and forcing them into a holdings model produces meaningless performance figures.
 
**FR-ACC-031 — Purchase capacity [COULD]**: Voluntary purchase capacity (Einkauf) may be recorded as a manually entered figure and surfaced as a savings-goal input. This is a planning feature, not a tax feature.
 
# 11. Currency Model
 
TrackMyWealth shall distinguish four currency concepts:
 
| Level | Meaning | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| User reporting currency | Currency for the user’s global consolidated view | CHF |
| Container currency | Currency for one institution’s summary | DKB = EUR |
| Account / transaction currency | Native/source currency of the underlying financial record | USD account / EUR transaction |
| Security price/listing currency | Currency in which a market price is quoted | AAPL listing in USD |
 
User reporting currency (e.g. CHF)
        ^
        |  FX conversion
Container currency (e.g. EUR)
        ^
        |  FX conversion if required
Account / Transaction / Price currency (source currency)
 
**FR-CUR-001 — User reporting currency [MUST]**: A reporting currency shall be selected for the user and used by the global consolidated view.
 
**FR-CUR-002 — Container currency [MUST]**: A container currency shall be selected when an institution is created.
 
**FR-CUR-003 — Preserve source currency [MUST]**: Currency conversion shall never overwrite original amounts or source currencies.
 
**FR-CUR-004 — Consolidated conversion [MUST]**: All consolidated monetary values shall be converted to the logged-in user’s reporting currency.
 
**FR-CUR-005 — Institution conversion [MUST]**: Institution Summary values shall be converted to the container currency.
 
**FR-CUR-006 — Historical FX [MUST]**: Historical reports shall use historically appropriate FX rates.
 
**FR-CUR-007 — FX provenance [MUST]**: Stored FX rates shall identify currency pair, date, rate and data source.
 
**FR-CUR-008 — Change reporting currency [MUST]**: Changing a reporting/container currency shall recalculate derived views without modifying source data.
 
**FR-CUR-009 — Initial currencies [MUST]**: CHF and EUR shall be first-class; the architecture shall support USD, GBP and additional currencies needed by portfolios.
 
**FR-CUR-010 — Single conversion step [MUST]**: Conversion from an account currency to the user reporting currency shall use the direct pair where available, and shall not chain through the container currency. Chained conversion compounds rounding error and can make a container summary and the consolidated view disagree on the same account.
 
**FR-CUR-011 — Conversion date convention [MUST]**: The methodology shall define, and document, which date’s rate applies to each figure class: transaction-date rate for realised flows, valuation-date rate for balances and holdings, period-end rate for closing positions. Mixing conventions silently is a common source of unexplainable differences.
 
**FR-CUR-012 — FX rate gaps [MUST]**: Where no rate exists for a required date (weekend, holiday), the last available prior rate shall be carried forward, and the substitution shall be visible on inspection (PR-011).
 
## 11.1 Currency attribution
 
**FR-CUR-020 — Return decomposition [SHOULD]**: Portfolio return shall be decomposable into local-currency return and FX contribution, so that a user can see how much of a result came from the asset and how much from the exchange rate.
 
## This is materially important for the target market — a Swiss user holding US equities experiences two distinct effects — and is absent from all four analysed competitors. It requires only that every cross-currency value already stores the FX rate applied (FR-CUR-007), so the cost is low if the data model is correct from the start and high if it is retrofitted.
 
# 12. Transaction Ledger
 
Transactions are a central source of truth for cash-flow, budgeting and investment analysis. Imported source data shall be preserved separately from normalized user/reporting fields.
 
**FR-TRX-001 — Transaction provenance [MUST]**: Every imported transaction shall retain its source/import origin.
 
**FR-TRX-002 — Transaction types [MUST]**: Support income, expense, transfer, deposit, withdrawal, buy, sell, dividend, interest, fee, refund, debt repayment, pension contribution, credit-card purchase, settlement, valuation adjustment and corporate action.
 
**FR-TRX-003 — Source preservation [MUST]**: User edits shall not destroy original imported transaction content.
 
**FR-TRX-004 — Duplicate detection [MUST]**: Repeated imports shall detect probable duplicates.
 
**FR-TRX-005 — Search/filter [MUST]**: Transactions shall be searchable/filterable by date, institution, account, amount, currency, category, type, merchant/description and owner.
 
**FR-TRX-006 — Split transactions [SHOULD]**: A transaction should support multiple category allocations where required.
 
**FR-TRX-007 — Append-only ledger [MUST]**: The transaction ledger shall be append-only. Corrections shall be recorded as new compensating or superseding entries rather than in-place mutation of the original record. This is what makes every derived figure reproducible and every import safely re-runnable. Section 52.2 defines the three provenance tiers governing what may be soft-deleted, what may only be voided, and what reopens a reconciliation.
 
**FR-TRX-008 — Trade date and settlement date [MUST]**: Investment transactions shall carry both trade date and settlement date where the source provides them. Performance is measured on trade date; cash balances move on settlement date. Collapsing the two produces small, persistent and very hard to diagnose discrepancies against broker statements.
 
**FR-TRX-009 — Stable external identity [MUST]**: Where the source supplies a stable identifier, it shall be stored and used to make repeat imports idempotent. Where it does not, duplicate detection shall fall back to a documented fuzzy match on date, amount, currency and description.
 
**FR-TRX-010 — Import batch identity [MUST]**: Every transaction shall record the import batch that created it, enabling batch-level rollback (FR-IMP-005).
 
## 12.1 Balances, holdings snapshots and reconciliation
 
**This subsection is the single most important addition to the baseline.** Transaction history alone is never complete: brokers supply limited history, transfers arrive without cost basis, and corporate actions are frequently missing. A ledger-only system therefore drifts away from reality and the user discovers it as an unexplained difference against their bank.
 
The competitive evidence is direct. Parqet’s significant product evolution was moving from transaction-only reconstruction to reconciling transactions against broker-reported holdings, entry prices and cash balances. getquin’s most-reported weakness is position counts and returns that disagree with the broker.
 
**FR-REC-001 — Snapshot is a distinct record type [MUST]**: The system shall store provider-reported point-in-time snapshots — account balance, holdings with quantities, and where available cost basis — as records distinct from transactions. A snapshot is an observation of state; a transaction is an event. Neither can be derived from the other.
 
**FR-REC-002 — Reconciliation engine [MUST]**: The system shall compare ledger-derived balances and holdings against the most recent snapshot and report differences per account, showing the amount, the affected position and the probable cause.
 
**FR-REC-003 — Probable-cause classification [SHOULD]**: Differences shall be classified where possible: missing transaction, missing corporate action, unrecorded fee, FX rounding, transfer without cost basis, duplicate entry.
 
**FR-REC-004 — Guided resolution [MUST]**: For each difference the user shall be offered explicit resolution paths — add the missing transaction, accept the provider figure as an adjusting entry, or dismiss with a reason. Adjustments shall be recorded as visible ledger entries, never as silent corrections.
 
**FR-REC-005 — Reconciliation status is visible [MUST]**: Each account shall display when it was last reconciled and whether it currently agrees with the provider. An unreconciled account’s figures shall be marked (PR-011).
 
**FR-REC-006 — Manual snapshot entry [MUST]**: Users shall be able to enter a snapshot manually — typing the balance shown on a statement. This makes reconciliation available before any connector exists and is the mechanism by which a fully manual user still gets correctness.
 
**FR-REC-007 — Opening-balance reconstruction [MUST]**: Where transaction history begins later than the account itself, the user shall be able to record a dated opening balance and opening holdings with cost basis. Without this, every account whose history predates the import shows wrong returns from the first day.
 
**FR-REC-008 — Transferred-in positions [MUST]**: A position transferred from another custodian typically arrives with no cost basis. The system shall accept a user-supplied cost basis, offer the historical market price at transfer date as a proposal, and mark the position’s cost basis as estimated until confirmed.
 
# 13. Cash-Account Categorization and Reporting Taxonomy
 
Cash-account transactions shall be categorized to support meaningful household reports. Standardized/source classification shall remain separate from TrackMyWealth’s reporting categories.
 
**FR-CAT-001 — Hierarchical categories [MUST]**: Reporting categories shall support at least Category > Subcategory.
 
**FR-CAT-002 — Default taxonomy [MUST]**: The application shall ship with a sensible household reporting taxonomy.
 
**FR-CAT-003 — Custom taxonomy [MUST]**: Users shall create, rename, reorganize and deactivate categories.
 
**FR-CAT-004 — ISO 20022 source classification [SHOULD]**: Where available, ISO 20022 Bank Transaction Codes or equivalent bank source codes shall be preserved.
 
**FR-CAT-005 — Standard-to-report mapping [MUST]**: Source classifications shall be mappable to TrackMyWealth reporting categories.
 
**FR-CAT-006 — User override [MUST]**: Users shall override reporting category without modifying source classification.
 
**FR-CAT-007 — Rules [MUST]**: Users shall define reusable categorization rules based on merchant/description, creditor/debtor, account, amount or source classification.
 
**FR-CAT-008 — Language-independent IDs [MUST]**: Default categories shall use stable internal IDs so English/German labels can change without changing historic classification.
 
**FR-CAT-009 — camt source codes [SHOULD]**: Where statements are supplied in ISO 20022 camt.052/053/054 format — the standard delivery format for Swiss and German banks — the structured bank transaction code (domain / family / sub-family) and purpose code shall be extracted and retained as the primary categorization signal for bank transactions.
 
**FR-CAT-010 — Shipped mapping as configuration [MUST]**: The default mapping from source codes (MCC, ISO 20022) to reporting categories shall be configuration data, updatable without an application release.
 
**FR-CAT-011 — Fallback categorization [MUST]**: Where no standard source code is present, categorization shall fall back to merchant name, counterparty identifier and amount pattern. This is not an edge case: source codes are frequently absent, and a card MCC often reflects the acquirer rather than the merchant.
 
**FR-CAT-012 — Retroactive rule application [MUST]**: A newly created or edited rule shall be applicable to existing historical transactions, with a preview of what would change before it is applied.
 
**FR-CAT-013 — Uncategorized is visible [MUST]**: Uncategorized transactions shall be surfaced as an actionable list. They shall never be silently absorbed into a residual “Other” bucket, which hides the gap and quietly distorts every category report.
 
**FR-CAT-014 — Recategorization is non-destructive [MUST]**: Re-running categorization shall never overwrite a user’s manual assignment.
 
**FR-CAT-015 — COICOP mapping [COULD]**: Reporting categories may be mapped to COICOP, the international classification of household consumption used by national statistical offices in both target markets. This enables comparison of household spending against published national averages by category — a differentiating report that no analysed competitor offers, and cheap to add once the mapping layer exists.
 
# 14. Credit Cards
 
**FR-CC-001 — Individual transactions [MUST]**: Credit-card accounts shall store individual purchases/transactions.
 
**FR-CC-002 — MCC [MUST]**: Where available, Merchant Category Code (ISO 18245) shall be retained separately from the reporting category.
 
**FR-CC-003 — Outstanding liability [MUST]**: Outstanding credit-card balance shall contribute to liabilities/net worth.
 
**FR-CC-004 — Settlement handling [MUST]**: Payment of the card balance shall be treated as liability settlement/internal transfer, not a second expense.
 
**FR-CC-005 — Double-count prevention [MUST]**: A credit-card purchase and later card settlement shall count as consumption exactly once.
 
**FR-CC-006 — Unified categories [MUST]**: Cash-account and credit-card transactions shall use the same reporting taxonomy.
 
**FR-CC-007 — Automatic settlement matching [MUST]**: The system shall detect the payment from a current account to a card issuer and match it to the corresponding card balance, proposing it as a settlement rather than an expense. The user shall be able to confirm or correct the match (FR-CF-004).
 
**FR-CC-008 — Statement cycle [SHOULD]**: Card accounts shall support statement period, closing balance, due date and paid/unpaid status.
 
**FR-CC-009 — Transaction-date attribution [MUST]**: Card spending shall be attributed to the transaction date, not the settlement date, so a purchase appears in the month it was made. Attributing to settlement date shifts a variable portion of every month’s spending into the next and makes month-on-month budget comparison meaningless.
 
**FR-CC-010 — Foreign-currency card transactions [SHOULD]**: The original amount, original currency and the rate applied by the issuer shall be retained. Where the issuer discloses a foreign-transaction fee it shall be identified as a fee rather than as part of the purchase amount.
 
**FR-CC-011 — Card utilisation alert [COULD]**: Optional notification on approaching due date or high utilisation.
 
# 15. Internal Transfers, Saving and Investing
 
**FR-CF-001 — Internal transfer neutrality [MUST]**: Transfers between owned accounts shall not increase income or expenses.
 
**FR-CF-002 — Investment classification [MUST]**: Transfers into investment accounts and security purchases shall not automatically be treated as consumer expenses.
 
**FR-CF-003 — Savings distinction [MUST]**: The ledger shall distinguish consumption, saving, investment, internal transfer and debt-principal repayment.
 
**FR-CF-004 — Manual correction [MUST]**: Users shall be able to confirm/correct transfer matching and classification.
 
**FR-CF-005 — Two-sided transfer matching [MUST]**: An internal transfer normally appears twice — as a debit in one account and a credit in another, often on different dates and occasionally in different currencies. The system shall match the two legs into a single logical transfer, and shall handle the case where only one leg has been imported.
 
**FR-CF-006 — Mortgage payment decomposition [SHOULD]**: A mortgage or loan payment shall be separable into interest (an expense) and principal repayment (a balance-sheet movement, not consumption). Treating the entire payment as an expense overstates spending and understates savings rate — a common and material error in household budgeting.
 
**FR-CF-007 — Pension contribution treatment [MUST]**: A contribution to a pension account is saving, not consumption, and shall be classified accordingly in cash-flow and savings-rate calculations.
 
# 16. Budgeting and Cash Flow
 
**FR-BUD-001 — Monthly budgets [MUST]**: Users shall define monthly category budgets.
 
**FR-BUD-002 — Budget progress [MUST]**: Show budgeted amount, actual amount, remaining amount and utilization.
 
**FR-BUD-003 — Household budget [MUST]**: Support household-level budgets.
 
**FR-BUD-004 — Savings rate [MUST]**: Calculate a savings rate from configurable classification rules.
 
**FR-BUD-005 — Recurring transactions [SHOULD]**: Support manual recurring items; automatic detection should follow later.
 
**FR-BUD-006 — Sankey cash flow [SHOULD]**: Provide a money-flow/Sankey visualization in a post-MVP phase.
 
**FR-BUD-007 — Budget proposal from history [SHOULD]**: When a user first creates budgets, the system shall propose category amounts derived from that household’s own actual transaction history rather than presenting empty fields. The design thesis, validated by BlueBudget, is that budgets entered as intentions fail while budgets derived from observed behaviour hold.
 
**FR-BUD-008 — Irregular and annual costs [SHOULD]**: Budgeting shall accommodate non-monthly obligations — insurance premiums, Serafe, annual subscriptions, tax instalments — by amortising them across months or flagging them as upcoming, so that a month containing one is not misread as an overspend.
 
**FR-BUD-009 — Savings rate methodology is documented and configurable [MUST]**: Whether employer pension contributions, mortgage principal and unrealised investment gains count toward savings shall be an explicit, documented and user-visible setting. Every published definition differs, and an undocumented choice makes the headline figure unusable.
 
# 17. Assets, Liabilities and Net Worth
 
| Assets | Liabilities |
| --- | --- |
| Cash | Mortgages |
| Stocks / ETFs / Funds / Bonds | Loans |
| Cryptocurrency | Credit-card balances |
| Pensions | Other manually defined liabilities |
| Real estate |  |
| Commodities / precious metals |  |
| Custom manually valued assets |  |
 
**FR-NW-001 — Net worth [MUST]**: Calculate total assets minus total liabilities.
 
**FR-NW-002 — Historical net worth [MUST]**: Provide historical net-worth development.
 
**FR-NW-003 — Manual valuations [MUST]**: Custom assets shall support dated valuations.
 
**FR-NW-004 — Scope breakdown [MUST]**: Net worth shall be analyzable by owner, institution, account, asset class and currency.
 
**FR-NW-005 — Contribution versus performance [MUST]**: Change in net worth over a period shall be decomposable into money added or withdrawn versus value gained or lost. Without this, a user who saved diligently during a falling market cannot tell that they did the right thing, and the headline number actively misleads.
 
**FR-NW-006 — Liability amortisation [SHOULD]**: Mortgages and loans shall support an amortisation schedule — principal, interest rate, fixation end date — so that the outstanding balance evolves correctly between manual updates rather than remaining static.
 
**FR-NW-007 — Property net of financing [SHOULD]**: Real estate shall be presentable at gross value with the related mortgage shown separately, and also at net value. The two must never be combined in a way that double-counts the debt.
 
**FR-NW-008 — Liquidity view [SHOULD]**: Net worth shall be viewable by accessibility — immediately available, available with notice, and locked. Pension assets are frequently the largest single component and are not spendable; presenting them without distinction overstates a household’s real flexibility.
 
# 18. Securities Accounts / Depots
 
A depot contains investment transactions and positions. Portfolio-specific information belongs to the position; security-independent reference data belongs to the centralized Security Master.
 
**FR-DEP-001 — Position reference [MUST]**: Every depot position shall reference a Security Master record.
 
**FR-DEP-002 — Position data [MUST]**: Position-specific data includes quantity, cost basis and calculated position values/performance.
 
**FR-DEP-003 — Cross-depot consolidation [MUST]**: The same security held in multiple depots shall consolidate into one security exposure while retaining separate positions.
 
**FR-DEP-004 — Cost-basis method [MUST]**: The cost-basis method shall be a configurable accounting parameter — FIFO, LIFO or average cost — defaulting to FIFO. Different providers report differently: some brokers report weighted average while the method used for reconstruction is FIFO, and the resulting divergence must be explainable to the user rather than appearing as an error.
 
**FR-DEP-005 — Fee treatment in cost basis [SHOULD]**: Whether transaction fees are included in or excluded from cost basis shall be configurable and documented. This is a known source of small persistent differences against broker statements.
 
**FR-DEP-006 — Position lots [SHOULD]**: Positions shall be decomposable into acquisition lots so that realised gain calculation is inspectable and reproducible under the configured method.
 
**FR-DEP-007 — Short and zero positions [SHOULD]**: The model shall handle a position reaching zero and later being re-established without losing the earlier history, and shall not silently produce negative quantities from an unmatched sale — such a case indicates missing history and shall raise a reconciliation difference (FR-REC-002).
 
# 19. Security Master Data
 
TrackMyWealth shall maintain a centralized Security Master only for securities referenced by current or historical portfolio activity. It shall not persist an unnecessary complete global security universe.
 
**FR-SMD-001 — Portfolio-driven master [MUST]**: Create a master record when a previously unknown security is first referenced by a portfolio transaction/position.
 
**FR-SMD-002 — Historical retention [MUST]**: Retain master data for securities no longer held when historical transactions/reports require it.
 
**FR-SMD-003 — Lifecycle [MUST]**: Support ACTIVE and INACTIVE/HISTORICAL security states.
 
**FR-SMD-004 — Single master [MUST]**: The same security held in several depots shall have one Security Master record.
 
**FR-SMD-005 — Deletion rule [MUST]**: A master record shall be deletable only when no transaction or position in any portfolio references it — never merely because nobody currently holds it.
 
**FR-SMD-006 — Refresh scope follows references [MUST]**: Price and reference-data refresh shall apply only to securities currently held or watchlisted, not to every security ever seen. This is the primary control on recurring external data cost and request volume.
 
**FR-SMD-007 — Lookup without persistence [MUST]**: Searching for a security at add-time may query an external universe without creating a master record; the record is written only on confirmed selection.
 
**FR-SMD-008 — Identity resolution [MUST]**: The same security arriving from different institutions under different identifiers — ISIN from one, Valor from another, ticker from a third — shall resolve to a single master record. ISIN is authoritative where present. A fallback match on ticker plus exchange shall require user confirmation and shall never be applied silently, because ticker collisions across markets are common.
 
**FR-SMD-009 — Effective-dated reference data [SHOULD]**: Names, domiciles and classifications change over time. Where a field materially affects reporting, its history shall be retained so that historical reports remain reproducible.
 
**FR-SMD-010 — Successor linkage [MUST]**: Identifier changes, mergers and spin-offs shall create an explicit link from predecessor to successor so that position history remains continuous across the event.
 
**FR-SMD-011 — Completeness indicator [MUST]**: Each master record shall expose which analysis-relevant fields are populated. Where a requested analysis depends on a missing field, the system shall say so rather than silently producing a chart that omits the position (PR-011).
 
**FR-SMD-012 — Field-level provenance [MUST]**: Each significant field shall record its source, retrieval timestamp and, where multiple providers disagree, the precedence rule applied.
 
**FR-SMD-013 — User override per field [MUST]**: A user-supplied value shall take precedence over provider data and shall never be silently overwritten by a later refresh.
 
## 19.1 Security Master fields
 
| Area | Required information |
| --- | --- |
| Identification | Internal Security ID, ISIN where available, additional identifiers, official/short name |
| Additional identifiers | **Valorennummer (CH) and WKN (DE) are not optional in these markets** — Swiss and German institutions routinely deliver them instead of ISIN. Also CUSIP, SEDOL, FIGI where available. |
| Description | Security description and company/issuer description, in EN and DE |
| Issuer | Issuer name, optional LEI/identifier, issuer details |
| Countries | Security country, issuer country, country of risk, exchange country, **withholding-source country** |
| Currencies | Denomination/security currency, trading currencies, price currency, **currency-hedged share class flag** |
| Instrument | Instrument type and CFI where available |
| Asset class | Economic portfolio asset class — **weighted, one-to-many for funds** (see FR-CLS-004) |
| GICS | Sector, Industry Group, Industry, Sub-Industry |
| SNB | Applicable SNB-oriented classification fields; exact taxonomy to be finalized |
| Listings | Exchange, MIC, ticker, country, trading currency, primary-listing flag |
| Prices | Historical price series with price type, currency, source and date |
| Fund attributes | Where applicable: TER, distributing/accumulating, replication method, share class, benchmark, look-through holdings |
| Bond attributes | Where applicable: coupon, maturity, payment frequency, rating, seniority |
| Provenance | Source/provider, retrieval date, effective date/version where relevant |
 
**Note on countries.** These are four genuinely different values and must be four fields. An Irish-domiciled ETF, issued by a Luxembourg entity, holding US equities, differs on three of them. The withholding-source country is retained to explain a reduced dividend receipt (FR-TAXR-002), not for reclaim logic.
 
# 20. Security, Issuer and Listing Separation
 
Security
+-- Issuer
+-- Classifications
+-- Listings [0..n]
    +-- Exchange / MIC
    +-- Ticker
    +-- Trading Currency
    +-- Price Series
 
A ticker belongs to a listing, not globally to the security. The same ISIN may have multiple exchange listings and trading currencies. Exchange-traded prices shall therefore normally be attached to a listing/price series.
 
**FR-LST-001 — Multiple listings [MUST]**: A security shall support multiple exchange listings.
 
**FR-LST-002 — MIC [MUST]**: Market Identifier Code (MIC) shall be stored where available.
 
**FR-LST-003 — Primary listing [SHOULD]**: A primary/preferred listing should be identifiable.
 
**FR-LST-004 — Valuation listing selection [MUST]**: Where a security has several listings, the rule determining which price is used for valuing a given position shall be explicit and inspectable — normally the listing on which the position was acquired, falling back to the primary listing. This case is common in the target market: the same ETF quoted on SIX in CHF and on XETRA in EUR.
 
**FR-LST-005 — Trading calendar [MUST]**: Each listing shall carry a trading calendar and timezone. Without it, a non-trading day cannot be distinguished from missing data, and the daily valuation series required for TWR (FR-PERF-003) becomes unreliable.
 
# 21. Asset and Industry Classification
 
## 21.1 Instrument type versus asset class
 
Instrument structure and economic asset class shall be separate. Example: Instrument Type = ETF, Economic Asset Class = Equity.
 
**FR-CLS-001 — Asset classes [MUST]**: Support Equity, Fixed Income, Cash/Cash Equivalent, Real Estate, Commodity, Precious Metal, Cryptocurrency and Other.
 
**FR-CLS-002 — CFI [MUST]**: Store CFI for instrument classification where available.
 
**FR-CLS-003 — CFI as the asset-class backbone [MUST]**: CFI (ISO 10962) shall be the primary source for asset-class assignment where available. It is an ISO standard tied to the security identifier and carries no licensing restriction, unlike sector data.
 
**FR-CLS-004 — A fund is a weighted set of asset classes, not one [MUST]**: The relationship between a position and asset class shall be one-to-many with weights. This is a data-model requirement in the MVP even where look-through data is not yet available, because a single asset-class field on a position makes correct allocation for a multi-asset or bond ETF impossible to express, and retrofitting it means rewriting every allocation query.
 
**FR-CLS-005 — Declared allocation fallback [SHOULD]**: Where constituent look-through is unavailable, a fund’s own published allocation may be used, and the result shall be marked as estimated (PR-011).
 
## 21.2 GICS
 
GICS shall be the canonical standard for grouping applicable corporate/equity exposure by sector and industry. GICS is a four-tier hierarchical system of 11 sectors, 25 industry groups, 74 industries and 163 sub-industries, developed by and the exclusive property of MSCI and S&P Dow Jones Indices.
 
**FR-GICS-001 — GICS hierarchy [MUST]**: Support Sector > Industry Group > Industry > Sub-Industry.
 
**FR-GICS-002 — Cross-depot grouping [MUST]**: GICS analysis shall work across one depot, one institution, selected depots, a person or the household portfolio.
 
**FR-GICS-003 — Applicability [MUST]**: Do not assign artificial GICS classifications to assets for which GICS is not applicable. GICS classifies companies, not instruments: it says nothing about bonds, commodities, crypto, cash or property. It is a grouping dimension inside the equity sleeve, layered on top of the asset class, never a replacement for it.
 
**FR-GICS-004 — ETF look-through [SHOULD]**: Where licensed constituent data is available, equity ETF/fund exposures should be decomposed into underlying GICS exposure.
 
**FR-GICS-005 — Funds carry no GICS code of their own [MUST]**: A fund’s sector exposure is only obtainable by weighted aggregation of its constituents. Until constituent data is licensed, sector analysis is unavailable for funds and shall be shown as such — not as an empty or misleading chart. This matters more than it appears: ETFs are the majority of a typical target user’s portfolio.
 
**FR-GICS-006 — Eight-digit code with derived levels [MUST]**: Store the full 8-digit sub-industry code and derive Industry, Industry Group and Sector by truncation, rather than storing four independent fields that can fall out of agreement.
 
**FR-GICS-007 — Structure versioning [MUST]**: The GICS structure is reviewed annually and revised — categories are added, split and renamed. The structure version shall be stored alongside the code.
 
**FR-GICS-008 — Historical basis is declared [SHOULD]**: Historical sector charts shall state whether they are shown as-reported (classification in force at the time) or restated (current classification applied throughout). Default restated. Mixing the two across a revision boundary produces charts that quietly contradict themselves.
 
**FR-GICS-009 — Not-classified bucket [MUST]**: Instruments with no GICS classification shall appear in an explicit, visible “Not classified” group — never dropped from the chart, which silently changes every percentage shown.
 
**FR-GICS-010 — Attribution notice [MUST]**: Display the attribution and trademark notice required by the licence wherever GICS data appears.
 
**FR-GICS-011 — Fallback taxonomy [MUST]**: If the GICS licence is unavailable, delayed or withdrawn, sector grouping shall degrade to a documented internal taxonomy without schema change or data loss. Given the self-hosted topology and open-source distribution, this is a realistic scenario rather than a theoretical one.
 
**NFR-GICS-001 — GICS licensing [MUST]**: The architecture shall separate proprietary GICS reference data from the open-source application core and respect licensing requirements.
 
**NFR-GICS-002 — Distribution boundary [MUST]**: No proprietary classification data shall be embedded in the distributed application package or in any publicly available repository. Licensed data shall be retrieved at runtime under the user’s or the operator’s own entitlement. See RISK-003 and OPEN-006.
 
## 21.3 SNB classification
 
**FR-SNB-001 — SNB field structure [MUST]**: Security Master shall support applicable SNB-oriented security/sector classification fields independently of GICS.
 
**FR-SNB-002 — Taxonomy validation [TBD]**: The exact SNB taxonomy/code list and data source shall be finalized before implementation.
 
**FR-SNB-003 — Orthogonality [MUST]**: SNB and GICS classifications are orthogonal and neither shall be derived from the other. GICS classifies a company’s industry; the SNB scheme classifies the issuer’s institutional sector and the instrument category for statistical reporting purposes. Both are stored, both are separately reportable.
 
# 22. Security Prices and Market Data
 
**FR-PRC-001 — Persistent prices [MUST]**: Store historical prices required for portfolio calculations for portfolio-referenced securities.
 
**FR-PRC-002 — Initial history [MUST]**: When a security enters a portfolio, retrieve sufficient history from the earliest relevant transaction date.
 
**FR-PRC-003 — Incremental updates [MUST]**: Active securities shall receive incremental price updates.
 
**FR-PRC-004 — Inactive securities [MUST]**: Historical prices remain stored after a position is closed; future updates may stop until reactivation.
 
**FR-PRC-005 — Price attributes [MUST]**: Store date/time, price, original currency, price type, source and retrieval timestamp.
 
**FR-PRC-006 — NAV/manual prices [MUST]**: Support NAV where appropriate and manual prices when automated data is unavailable.
 
**FR-PRC-007 — Corporate actions [MUST]**: Valuation/performance logic shall account for relevant splits, mergers, spin-offs and identifier changes.
 
**FR-PRC-008 — Gap handling [MUST]**: Define deterministic handling for weekends, holidays, suspensions and missing prices.
 
**FR-PRC-009 — Raw prices are immutable; adjusted series are derived [MUST]**: Prices shall be stored exactly as delivered, with corporate actions stored separately, and any split- or dividend-adjusted series computed on read. Cost basis and realised gains require the price actually traded on the day. A provider that retroactively adjusts a stored series corrupts historical accounting irreversibly.
 
**FR-PRC-010 — Price backfill on first reference [MUST]**: When a security first enters the system, its price history shall be backfilled to the earliest referencing transaction date. Without this, TWR, historical net worth and allocation charts have no data covering the period before the user adopted the application.
 
**FR-PRC-011 — Carry-forward, never interpolate [MUST]**: Across non-trading days the last known price shall be carried forward. Values shall never be interpolated between two known points, which invents price movement that did not occur.
 
**FR-PRC-012 — Staleness threshold [MUST]**: A price older than a configurable threshold shall be flagged as stale in every view that uses it (PR-011, NFR-CON-003).
 
**FR-PRC-013 — FX rates as a parallel series [MUST]**: FX rates shall be stored as their own dated time series per currency pair, under the same provenance, gap and staleness rules as prices.
 
**FR-PRC-014 — Provider abstraction with precedence [MUST]**: Price and FX sources shall sit behind an interface supporting more than one implementation, with configurable global precedence and per-security override.
 
**FR-PRC-015 — Corporate-action review [SHOULD]**: Automatically applied corporate actions shall be visible and reversible. A wrongly applied split silently multiplies or divides a position and is extremely difficult for a user to diagnose unaided.
 
# 23. Portfolio Performance and Analytics
 
**FR-PERF-001 — Absolute performance [MUST]**: Calculate absolute gain/loss.
 
**FR-PERF-002 — Realized/unrealized [MUST]**: Calculate realized and unrealized gains/losses.
 
**FR-PERF-003 — TWR [MUST]**: Calculate Time-Weighted Return for suitable investment scopes.
 
**FR-PERF-004 — MWR [MUST]**: Calculate Money-Weighted Return, using IRR/XIRR methodology where appropriate.
 
**FR-PERF-005 — Periods [MUST]**: Support YTD, 1M, 1Y, 3Y, 5Y, max and custom date ranges as applicable.
 
**FR-PERF-006 — Scope [MUST]**: Performance shall work at position, depot, institution, selected portfolios, owner and household-investment levels where meaningful.
 
**FR-PERF-007 — Benchmarking [MUST]**: Allow comparison with selectable benchmarks.
 
**FR-PERF-008 — Explanations [SHOULD]**: Advanced metrics should include beginner-friendly explanations.
 
## 23.1 Methodology requirements
 
**FR-PERF-010 — Daily valuation series [MUST]**: The system shall maintain a daily valuation series per investment account. **This is an architectural prerequisite, not a reporting feature.** TWR requires sub-period returns bounded by every external cash flow; MWR can be computed on demand from cash flows, but TWR cannot. If daily valuations are not built in the MVP, adding TWR later requires a full historical recomputation for every user.
 
**FR-PERF-011 — TWR construction [MUST]**: TWR shall be computed by geometrically linking sub-period returns, with sub-periods bounded by external cash flows into or out of the scope being measured. Internal movements — a purchase funded by cash already inside the scope — are not external cash flows and must not break the sub-period.
 
**FR-PERF-012 — Approximation is declared [MUST]**: Where an exact valuation at a cash-flow date is unavailable, a documented approximation (such as Modified Dietz) may be used, and the method actually applied shall be recorded for that period and inspectable by the user.
 
**FR-PERF-013 — Simple return remains primary for beginners [MUST]**: Absolute gain/loss and simple return shall remain available and shall be the default headline for non-expert users. TWR and MWR are additive depth, consistent with PR-006, and must not displace the figure most users actually want.
 
**FR-PERF-014 — Annualised versus cumulative [MUST]**: Annualised and cumulative variants shall be clearly distinguished and never presented interchangeably. Annualising a period shorter than one year shall be avoided or explicitly labelled.
 
**FR-PERF-015 — Benchmark parity [MUST]**: A benchmark comparison shall apply the same methodology and the same cash-flow timing as the portfolio figure it is compared against. Comparing a cash-flow-weighted portfolio return to a simple index return is not a like-for-like comparison and overstates or understates skill depending on market direction.
 
**FR-PERF-016 — Explain this number [SHOULD]**: Every performance figure shall be able to show the method used, the period boundaries, the cash flows applied, the valuation source and any data gaps encountered. This is the practical expression of PR-005 and PR-011, and is the single most effective defence against the loss of trust that follows an unexplained number.
 
**FR-PERF-017 — Currency attribution [SHOULD]**: Where the measurement scope contains assets in currencies other than the reporting currency, return shall be decomposable into local-currency return and FX contribution (FR-CUR-020).
 
**FR-PERF-018 — Insufficient data is refused, not estimated [MUST]**: Where history is too incomplete for a requested metric to be meaningful — no opening cost basis, unreconciled gap, missing prices across a material period — the system shall state that the figure cannot be reliably calculated rather than presenting a computed but wrong value (PR-010).
 
**FR-PERF-019 — Default metric by account nature [SHOULD]**: Discretionary accounts shall default to TWR and self-directed accounts to MWR (FR-ACC-022), with user override.
 
# 24. Investment Allocation
 
**FR-ALL-001 — Asset class [MUST]**: Analyze by economic asset class.
 
**FR-ALL-002 — Security [MUST]**: Analyze by individual security.
 
**FR-ALL-003 — GICS [MUST]**: Analyze by GICS levels where applicable.
 
**FR-ALL-004 — Country [SHOULD]**: Analyze geographic exposure where sufficient data exists.
 
**FR-ALL-005 — Currency [MUST]**: Analyze currency exposure.
 
**FR-ALL-006 — Institution/account [MUST]**: Analyze by institution/container and depot/account.
 
**FR-ALL-007 — Direct/indirect exposure [SHOULD]**: Where look-through data exists, distinguish direct holdings from indirect ETF/fund exposure.
 
**FR-ALL-008 — Pension holdings included in allocation [MUST]**: Where a pension account holds positions — a VIAC Pillar 3a being the canonical case — those holdings shall be included in consolidated asset allocation, currency exposure and concentration analysis on the same taxonomy as any other holding.
 
This is differentiation pillar D2. Swiss users routinely hold a large equity allocation inside a Pillar 3a, and no analysed competitor folds it into the overall picture. Concentration analysis that excludes it is not merely incomplete for these users — it is wrong.
 
**FR-ALL-009 — Scope is always visible [MUST]**: The active scope of any allocation view — which accounts, which owner, whether pensions are included — shall be displayed alongside the chart. A user must never be uncertain whether a figure includes their pension.
 
**FR-ALL-010 — Concentration analysis [SHOULD]**: Identify single-position and single-issuer concentration above configurable thresholds, computed after look-through where available, so that the same company held directly and inside three ETFs is recognised as one exposure.
 
## **FR-ALL-011 — Drill-down to classification source [SHOULD]**: Allocation views shall be drillable from asset class or sector down to the individual security and the classification record that placed it there.
 
# 25. Dividends
 
**FR-DIV-001 — Dividend transactions [MUST]**: Recognize dividends as a distinct investment transaction type.
 
**FR-DIV-002 — History [MUST]**: Provide dividend history by security, account, institution and period.
 
**FR-DIV-003 — Calendar/forecast [SHOULD]**: Expected dividend calendar/forecast may be added where reliable data exists.
 
**FR-DIV-004 — Gross and net [MUST]**: Where tax was deducted at source, both the gross dividend and the amount actually received shall be distinguishable (FR-TAXR-001). Dividend yield and income analysis shall state which basis they use.
 
**FR-DIV-005 — Yield on cost [SHOULD]**: Dividend yield shall be available both on current market value and on original cost.
 
**FR-DIV-006 — Accumulating funds [MUST]**: Accumulating funds distribute nothing. They shall not appear in dividend income reports, and their absence shall not be presented as missing data.
 
No tax-oriented dividend analysis is in scope.
 
# 25a. Pension and Retirement
 
Pension is in scope as a financial account category and as a savings goal. It is not in scope as tax optimisation (OOS-004).
 
**FR-PEN-001 — Pension as account type [MUST]**: Pension accounts shall be first-class accounts within the container model, with locked-until or withdrawal-condition semantics reflected in the liquidity view (FR-NW-008).
 
**FR-PEN-002 — Multiple Pillar 3a accounts [MUST]**: A household may hold several Pillar 3a accounts, at one provider or several. This is standard Swiss practice for staggered withdrawal and is a normal case, not an edge case (rule C4).
 
**FR-PEN-003 — Contribution limit tracking [SHOULD]**: Annual contribution progress against the applicable limit shall be trackable, with the limit held as effective-dated configuration data rather than in code. This is a savings-goal feature: it shows whether a savings target has been met, and makes no tax statement.
 
**FR-PEN-004 — Contribution deadline reminder [SHOULD]**: Optional notification ahead of the annual contribution deadline.
 
**FR-PEN-005 — Position-holding pension accounts [MUST]**: Where a pension account holds securities, it shall support full position, valuation and performance treatment identical to a depot (FR-ACC-011, FR-ALL-008).
 
**FR-PEN-006 — Jurisdiction rules as configuration [MUST]**: Contribution limits, deadlines and account variants for CH and DE shall be effective-dated configuration, deployable without an application release.
 
**FR-PEN-007 — Retirement projection [COULD]**: Projected capital at retirement, based on current assets and contribution rate, with user-editable assumptions and results presented as scenario ranges rather than a single figure. Any such output is illustrative modelling, never advice (OOS-003).
 
**FR-PEN-008 — Financial independence modelling [COULD]**: Time-to-independence and sustainable withdrawal modelling for the financial-independence persona, under the same assumption-transparency rules.
 
# 26. Consolidated View
 
The global Consolidated View is the top-level financial overview and shall present all monetary aggregates in the logged-in user’s reporting currency.
 
- Net Worth
 
- Assets and Liabilities
 
- Cash
 
- Investments
 
- Pensions
 
- Real Estate
 
- Mortgages, loans and credit-card liabilities
 
- Income and Expenses
 
- Savings and Savings Rate
 
- Cash Flow
 
- Asset Allocation
 
- Portfolio Performance
 
- TWR and MWR
 
- Dividends
 
- Financial Goals
 
**FR-CON-001 — Single reporting currency [MUST]**: All consolidated monetary aggregates shall be displayed in the user’s reporting currency.
 
**FR-CON-002 — Historical conversion [MUST]**: Historical consolidated values shall use historical FX rates.
 
**FR-CON-003 — Global scope selector [MUST]**: Reports shall support scope by household, person, institution, account, selected accounts or investments only.
 
**FR-CON-004 — Date range [MUST]**: Relevant reports shall support date-range filtering.
 
**FR-CON-005 — Drill-down [MUST]**: Users shall drill from global values to institution, account, security/category and transaction.
 
**FR-CON-006 — Consistent scope across views [MUST]**: The selected scope shall apply consistently across all views and remain visible at all times, so that two figures on screen are never computed over different scopes without the user knowing.
 
**FR-CON-007 — Data-quality banner [MUST]**: Where the consolidated view includes unreconciled accounts, stale prices or estimated valuations, this shall be indicated at the level of the headline figure, not only in a detail view (PR-011).
 
**FR-CON-008 — Reconciliation of the whole [SHOULD]**: The sum of all Institution Summary net values, converted to the reporting currency, shall equal the consolidated net worth. Any residual arising from conversion timing shall be identified rather than absorbed.
 
# 27. Example Application Structure
 
TrackMyWealth
|
+-- Overview (User Reporting Currency: CHF)
|   +-- Net Worth
