*Change from the baseline: real estate and mortgage are shown inside a Personal Assets container rather than attached directly to the household, so that rule C2 (every account has exactly one parent container) holds without exception. See OPEN-012.*
 
# 28. Import and Integration Requirements
 
**FR-IMP-001 — Manual input [MUST]**: Support manual transaction/holding input.
 
**FR-IMP-002 — Multi-institution CSV import [MUST]**: Support configurable CSV import for **multiple different banks and brokers concurrently**. Each supported institution has its own export format; the importer shall handle an arbitrary number of such formats side by side without any of them being privileged in code.
 
**FR-IMP-003 — Mapping templates [MUST]**: Save reusable institution/import mappings. A template is the unit in which support for one institution’s CSV export is expressed.
 
**FR-IMP-004 — Preview [MUST]**: Show new records, duplicates, errors and warnings before commit.
 
**FR-IMP-005 — Batch/rollback [MUST]**: Each import shall be identifiable and reversible as a batch.
 
**FR-IMP-006 — PDF import [SHOULD]**: Institution-specific PDF parsers may be added where valuable.
 
**FR-IMP-007 — Connector abstraction [MUST]**: Automatic bank/broker connectors shall use a stable abstraction layer.
 
**FR-IMP-008 — Read-only integrations [MUST]**: Where automatic institution connections are added, they should be read-only because payment/trade execution is out of scope.
 
**FR-IMP-009 — Shipped import templates [MUST]**: Import mappings for the supported Swiss and German institutions shall ship as configuration data and be updatable without an application release. Users shall be able to share, export and import mapping definitions.
 
**FR-IMP-010 — Snapshot import [MUST]**: The import pipeline shall accept holdings and balance statements as snapshots (FR-REC-001), not only transaction lists. Many providers export current holdings far more readily than complete transaction history.
 
**FR-IMP-011 — camt.053 import [SHOULD]**: Native support for ISO 20022 camt.052/053/054 files, the standard bank statement format in both target markets, including structured transaction and purpose codes (FR-CAT-009).
 
**FR-IMP-012 — Partial import success [MUST]**: An import containing some unparseable rows shall import the valid rows and report the rest, rather than failing wholesale. The rejected rows shall be exportable for correction and re-import.
 
**FR-IMP-013 — Import provenance retained [MUST]**: The original source file or payload shall be retained, or at minimum a reference to it plus the raw parsed row, so that any imported record can be traced back to its origin.
 
**FR-IMP-014 — Connector health [MUST]**: Where automatic connectors exist, each shall expose a health state. A degraded connector shall be surfaced with a plain-language explanation and a manual fallback path, and shall never block access to already-imported data (PR-012).
 
**FR-IMP-015 — Connector fragility is assumed [MUST]**: Institution-side changes will break connectors without notice; this is an operating assumption, not an exception. Competitors have experienced multi-week outages from a single broker backend change. No feature shall be designed such that its correctness depends on a connector being currently functional.
 
## 28.1 Bank-specific CSV templates
 
Support for each individual bank or broker is delivered as a **declarative template**, not as institution-specific code. This is what allows the supported institution list to grow without touching the application.
 
**FR-IMP-020 — Template is a data artifact [MUST]**: An import template shall be a declarative definition held as configuration data. Adding, correcting or removing support for one institution shall require no code change and no application release.
 
**FR-IMP-021 — Template capability set [MUST]**: A template shall be able to express at minimum the following, because Swiss and German bank exports vary on every one of them:
 
| Aspect | Variation the template must handle |
| --- | --- |
| Delimiter | Comma, semicolon (common in DE/CH exports), tab, pipe |
| Character encoding | UTF-8, UTF-8 with BOM, ISO-8859-1, Windows-1252 |
| Decimal and thousands separators | 1234.56, 1.234,56 (DE), 1'234.56 (CH) |
| Date format | ISO, DD.MM.YYYY, DD/MM/YYYY, two-digit years |
| Header structure | Header row position, preamble rows carrying account metadata, trailing summary rows to ignore |
| Amount representation | Single signed column, separate debit and credit columns, sign convention, negatives in parentheses |
| Currency | Fixed per file, per row, or derived from the account |
| Column mapping | Booking date, value date, trade date, description, counterparty, reference, amount, fee, tax, balance |
| Securities fields | ISIN, Valor, WKN, ticker, quantity, unit price, fee — where the export is a depot statement |
| Type mapping | Source transaction-type strings mapped to the canonical transaction types (section 12) |
| Account identification | How the target account is recognised — from a column, a preamble line, the filename, or chosen by the user |
 
**FR-IMP-022 — Template selection [MUST]**: On import the system shall attempt to identify the correct template automatically, for example by header fingerprint, and shall always allow the user to override the choice or select a template manually.
 
**FR-IMP-023 — Template versioning [MUST]**: Templates shall be versioned with an effective date. Institutions change their export format without notice, and a historical import must remain reproducible under the template version that was actually applied. The template version used shall be recorded on the import batch.
 
**FR-IMP-024 — User-defined templates [MUST]**: Where no shipped template exists, the user shall be able to define one through the mapping interface and save it for reuse. No institution is therefore ever unsupported; it is only more or less convenient (FR-INS-010).
 
**FR-IMP-025 — Unmapped columns preserved [MUST]**: Columns not referenced by a template shall be retained as raw source data rather than discarded, so that a later template revision can make use of them without a re-import (FR-IMP-013).
 
**FR-IMP-026 — Template test fixture [MUST]**: Every shipped template shall be accompanied by an anonymised sample file and its expected parse result, executed as an automated test. A template without a fixture is not considered delivered. This extends the verification dataset in section 47.
 
**FR-IMP-027 — Template failure is contained [MUST]**: A template that no longer matches the institution’s current export shall fail with a clear diagnostic naming the mismatch, and shall not partially import misaligned data. Silent column drift is more damaging than a refused import (PR-010).
 
### 28.1.1 Deferred input — institution list and templates
 
**The list of banks and brokers to be supported, together with their CSV templates and sample files, will be supplied during user-story creation.** They are deliberately not enumerated in this specification.
 
Implications for planning and handover:
 
- **INPUT-001**: For each institution in scope, the following shall be delivered with its user story — institution name and country, an anonymised sample export, the field mapping, and the expected parse result to be used as the test fixture (FR-IMP-026).
 
- **INPUT-002**: Each supported institution shall be its own user story under EPIC 07, sized independently. Institution support is incremental and additive; none of it sits on the critical path of the import framework itself.
 
- **INPUT-003**: The import framework (FR-IMP-001 to FR-IMP-027) is MVP scope and shall be built and tested against at least two structurally different sample formats — one Swiss, one German — before any institution-specific work begins, so that the abstraction is validated rather than assumed.
 
- **INPUT-004**: Depot and securities exports shall be treated as a distinct template class from cash-account exports, and at least one of each shall be represented in the initial set.
 
- **INPUT-005**: The number of institutions supported at launch is a scope decision to be taken with the delivered list, not a requirement of this document.
 
# 29. Data Provenance and Auditability
 
**FR-DAT-001 — Transaction provenance [MUST]**: Retain original transaction/import source.
 
**FR-DAT-002 — Reference-data provenance [MUST]**: Retain provider/retrieval/effective-date metadata for important security master fields.
 
**FR-DAT-003 — Price provenance [MUST]**: Every price series/observation shall identify its source.
 
**FR-DAT-004 — Classification provenance [MUST]**: GICS/SNB/CFI/MCC/ISO 20022 source information shall retain source/version where applicable.
 
**FR-DAT-005 — Admin audit [SHOULD]**: Important user-administration actions should be auditable.
 
**FR-DAT-006 — Three-layer classification model [MUST]**: All classification shall follow the same layering — source codes as received (immutable), the application’s canonical taxonomy (the only layer product logic reads), and optional mappings out to external reporting standards. Binding reports directly to a source standard means a provider change or a licence lapse breaks the reports; the canonical layer absorbs that churn.
 
**FR-DAT-007 — Classification is annotation, never mutation [MUST]**: Classifying a record shall never alter the record. Source values are preserved so that a full re-classification remains possible when rules improve; without this, an early mapping error is permanently baked into the user’s history.
 
**FR-DAT-008 — Derived data is rebuildable [MUST]**: All derived artefacts — positions, tax lots, daily valuations, aggregates — shall be fully reconstructible from the stored source records. Nothing derived is a source of truth.
 
# 30. Privacy, Security and Offline Operation
 
**NFR-PRIV-001 — Data location follows the deployment [MUST]**: Financial data resides in the PostgreSQL database of the deployment the user connects to. In a self-hosted deployment that is infrastructure the user controls; in the hosted service it is the operator’s infrastructure in a declared jurisdiction (section 51).
 
**NFR-PRIV-002 — No dependency on the operator [MUST]**: A self-hosted deployment shall be fully functional without any account, licence check, telemetry call or other runtime dependency on a service operated by the project. Optional outbound calls — market data, FX, classification — shall be individually configurable and disableable.
 
**NFR-PRIV-003 — Explicit consent [MUST]**: External synchronization or processing of financial data shall require explicit user consent.
 
**NFR-SEC-001 — Encryption at rest [MUST]**: Sensitive financial data shall be encrypted at rest.
 
**NFR-SEC-002 — Encryption in transit [MUST]**: External network communication containing sensitive data shall be encrypted.
 
**NFR-SEC-003 — Secure credentials [MUST]**: Secrets/credentials shall never be stored in plaintext and should use OS secure storage where available.
 
**NFR-SEC-004 — Application access protection [MUST]**: Support local access protection such as password/PIN/biometrics as appropriate to platform.
 
**NFR-SEC-005 — No credential enabling money movement [MUST]**: The system shall never store credentials permitting order placement or payment initiation, consistent with PR-007. This is a storage prohibition, not only a feature exclusion.
 
**NFR-SEC-006 — Outbound request minimisation [MUST]**: External requests shall disclose no more than the identifier required. Querying prices for a list of securities reveals a user’s portfolio to the provider; where feasible this shall be batched or padded, and it shall be documented in the privacy statement either way.
 
**NFR-CON-001 — Online-only client [MUST]**: The client requires connectivity to its API. Offline data entry, offline calculation and offline editing are explicitly out of scope. There is no client-side write queue and no synchronisation or conflict-resolution mechanism.
 
**NFR-CON-002 — Connectivity loss is handled, not ignored [MUST]**: Loss of connectivity mid-session shall be detected and communicated clearly. In-progress user input shall not be silently discarded; a failed write shall be retryable without duplicating the record (see idempotency, FR-API-006).
 
**NFR-CON-003 — Stale market data indicator [MUST]**: If the server cannot refresh prices or FX from its providers, it shall continue serving the last stored values and mark them stale everywhere they appear. This concerns provider availability, not client connectivity, and remains a requirement in both topologies (PR-011).
 
**NFR-CON-004 — Manual-price mode remains supported [MUST]**: A deployment with no external market-data entitlement at all shall remain fully usable with manually maintained prices. This is the baseline configuration for self-hosting (section 48.2).
 
## 30.1 Consequences of open distribution for licensed data
 
Open-source distribution combined with the self-hosted topology creates a specific structural problem that must be resolved before implementation. It is recorded here rather than left implicit. Section 48.2 resolves it per topology.
 
A locally installed, openly distributed application still requires market prices, FX rates and — under the GICS decision — licensed classification data. That data is obtained under commercial terms that typically restrict redistribution and require an identified licensee.
 
**NFR-LIC-001 — No embedded provider credentials [MUST]**: API keys or provider credentials shall not be embedded in the distributed application or its source repository. Any such key is effectively published, is a licence breach, and will be revoked.
 
**NFR-LIC-002 — Credential ownership model [MUST]**: The product shall define and document how each user obtains entitlement to external data — the user supplies their own key, an optional managed service proxies requests under the operator’s licence, or a free provider is used within its terms. This decision determines whether a fully local, fully free installation is functionally complete or degraded (OPEN-013).
 
**NFR-LIC-003 — Licensed data not redistributed [MUST]**: Licensed reference data shall not be shipped in the application package, in a seeded database, or in any public repository (NFR-GICS-002).
 
**NFR-LIC-004 — Documented degraded mode [MUST]**: The behaviour of the application with no external data entitlement at all — manual prices only — shall be a documented, supported and usable configuration, not an error state.
 
# 31. Data Ownership and Backup
 
## 31.1 Export — deferred
 
**User-facing data export is not a requirement at present (OOS-008).** No export screen, no download, no scheduled export. Deferred to post-MVP.
 
## 31.2 The residual obligation
 
One element cannot be deferred, and it is a legal obligation rather than a product feature.
 
**FR-POR-001 — Statutory data portability, hosted topology [MUST]**: Where TrackMyWealth is operated as a hosted service for residents of Switzerland or the EU, the operator is a data controller and must be able to supply a data subject’s personal data in a structured, commonly used, machine-readable form on request, within the statutory deadline (NFR-DPR-003).
 
*This does not require a user-facing feature.* It may be satisfied by an operator-side administrative procedure — a documented, tested extraction run by the operator on request. That is materially cheaper than a product feature and is the recommended MVP interpretation.
 
**FR-POR-002 — Not applicable to self-hosting [MUST]**: A self-hosting household is its own controller and has direct database access, so no portability mechanism is required from the application in that topology (NFR-DPR-008).
 
**FR-POR-003 — Schema documentation [MUST]**: The database schema shall be documented to a standard sufficient for the extraction in FR-POR-001 to be performed reliably. This documentation is required in any case for the architect and for self-hosting operators.
 
**Design note.** Because export is deferred rather than prohibited, no design decision may make it unreasonably expensive to add later. In practice this costs nothing: the requirements already mandate that all derived data be reconstructible from source records (FR-DAT-008) and that provenance be retained throughout (section 29). A schema satisfying those can be exported whenever the decision is reversed. See OPEN-031.
 
## 31.3 Backup and restore
 
Backup is a separate concern from export and remains fully in scope.
 
**FR-BCK-001 — Backup [MUST]**: Provide backup of the deployment’s data.
 
**FR-BCK-002 — Restore [MUST]**: Provide restore from backup.
 
**FR-BCK-003 — Encrypted backup [MUST]**: Sensitive backups shall support encryption.
 
**FR-BCK-004 — Restore verification [SHOULD]**: The application shall be able to verify a backup’s integrity and completeness without performing a destructive restore.
 
**FR-BCK-005 — Backup responsibility is explicit [MUST]**: Backup responsibility differs by topology and shall be stated in-product. In a self-hosted deployment the operator of that deployment carries the durability risk and shall be prompted, with the date of the last successful backup shown. In the hosted service, backup is the service operator’s responsibility under the targets in section 51. **With export deferred, a self-hosting household’s only protection against data loss is its own backup, which raises the importance of this requirement.**
 
**FR-BCK-006 — Backup is deployment-level, not tenant-level [MUST]**: With export deferred, backup operates on the deployment as a whole rather than on an individual household. In a multi-tenant hosted deployment, backup and restore therefore cannot serve as a per-household data-retrieval mechanism, which is why FR-POR-001 exists separately.
 
# 32. Localization and UX
 
**NFR-I18N-001 — English/German [MUST]**: All core system-generated UI content shall be available in English and German.
 
**NFR-I18N-002 — Runtime switching [MUST]**: Users shall change language without reinstalling.
 
**NFR-I18N-003 — Locale formatting [MUST]**: Dates, numbers, currencies and percentages shall follow locale preferences, including the Swiss thousands separator convention, which differs from the German one.
 
**NFR-I18N-004 — No concatenated sentences [MUST]**: User-facing text shall be externalised as complete units with parameters, never assembled from fragments, so that a further language can be added without code change.
 
**NFR-I18N-005 — Financial terminology per market [SHOULD]**: German-language terminology shall respect Swiss and German usage where they differ.
 
**NFR-UX-001 — Progressive disclosure [MUST]**: Advanced analytics shall not overwhelm beginner users.
 
**NFR-UX-002 — Consistent terminology [MUST]**: Institution/Container, Account, Transaction, Security, Position, Asset, Liability and Household shall have consistent meanings.
 
**NFR-UX-003 — Explainability [SHOULD]**: Users should be able to understand the source/calculation behind important figures.
 
**NFR-UX-004 — Estimated values are visually distinct [MUST]**: The interface shall carry a consistent visual treatment for estimated, stale and unreconciled values, applied everywhere such a value appears (PR-011).
 
**NFR-UX-005 — Time to first insight [SHOULD]**: A new user importing a single account shall reach a meaningful overview within ten minutes.
 
# 33. Financial Calculation and Reliability
 
**NFR-CALC-001 — Decimal arithmetic [MUST]**: Use appropriate decimal/financial arithmetic for monetary values. Binary floating point shall not be used for any monetary or quantity value at any point, including intermediate calculation.
 
**NFR-CALC-002 — Reproducibility [MUST]**: Same input and methodology shall produce the same result.
 
**NFR-CALC-003 — Document calculations [MUST]**: Formally document net worth, savings rate, FX, portfolio valuation, TWR and MWR methodologies. See section 46.
 
**NFR-CALC-004 — Automated tests [MUST]**: Calculation engines shall have comprehensive automated tests.
 
**NFR-CALC-005 — Golden dataset regression [MUST]**: A fixed reference dataset with known correct answers shall be maintained and run on every change to any calculation path. It shall include, at minimum: a stock split, a reverse split, a merger, a spin-off, an ISIN change, an in-kind transfer with no reported cost basis, a partial sale under FIFO, a multi-currency position, a dividend with withholding deducted, a mid-period cash flow affecting TWR, and an account whose transaction history begins after the account was opened. See section 47.
 
**NFR-CALC-006 — Known-answer tests for TWR and MWR [MUST]**: TWR, MWR and any approximation method shall be validated against published worked examples with independently verifiable results, not only against the system’s own prior output.
 
**NFR-CALC-007 — Rounding policy [MUST]**: A single documented rounding policy shall apply — where rounding occurs, to how many places, and per currency. Rounding shall be applied at presentation, not accumulated through intermediate steps.
 
**NFR-CALC-008 — Recomputation is idempotent [MUST]**: Recalculating derived data from unchanged source records shall produce identical results.
 
**NFR-REL-001 — Atomic updates [MUST]**: Multi-record financial operations/imports shall not leave partially committed states.
 
**NFR-REL-002 — Integrity constraints [MUST]**: Institution/account/transaction/holding relationships shall be protected by data-integrity rules enforced at the data layer, not only in application code.
 
**NFR-REL-003 — Schema migration safety [MUST]**: Migrations shall never silently relocate or alter user-visible balances. Where a structural change moves where a value appears, the change shall be explained in-app. A competitor’s mid-2026 restructuring of cash accounts demonstrated the cost of doing this silently.
 
# 34. Initial Performance Targets
 
| Area | Initial target |
| --- | --- |
| Main dashboard | Approximately < 1 second for a typical local household dataset after required data is loaded |
| Transaction filtering/search | Approximately < 500 ms for ~100,000 transactions on supported hardware |
| Common portfolio calculations | Approximately < 2 seconds for normal private-investor datasets |
| Import preview | Approximately < 5 seconds for ~10,000 records |
| Full historical recomputation | Approximately < 60 seconds for a large household dataset, as a background task with progress indication |
| Daily valuation series build | Incremental daily update shall not block interactive use |
 
These are engineering targets to be validated during prototyping rather than contractual service levels.
 
# 35. Core Business Rules
 
**RULE-001 [MANDATORY]**: A Financial Institution is a container, not an account.
 
**RULE-002 [MANDATORY]**: A container may hold multiple account types.
 
**RULE-003 [MANDATORY]**: Every container has a container currency and an Institution Summary.
 
**RULE-004 [MANDATORY]**: Each user has a reporting currency for the global Consolidated View.
 
**RULE-005 [MANDATORY]**: Source/account/security currencies remain unchanged by reporting conversions.
 
**RULE-006 [MANDATORY]**: Historical consolidated reports use historical FX rates.
 
**RULE-007 [MANDATORY]**: Internal transfers do not count as income or expenses.
 
**RULE-008 [MANDATORY]**: Security purchases do not automatically count as household consumption.
 
**RULE-009 [MANDATORY]**: Credit-card purchases count as expenses once; settlement does not create a second expense.
 
**RULE-010 [MANDATORY]**: Source transaction classification and reporting category remain separate.
 
**RULE-011 [MANDATORY]**: MCC remains separate from reporting category.
 
**RULE-012 [MANDATORY]**: Security Master information is centralized and referenced by depot positions.
 
**RULE-013 [MANDATORY]**: Only securities referenced by current/historical portfolios are persisted in the Security Master.
 
**RULE-014 [MANDATORY]**: Security and exchange listing are separate entities.
 
**RULE-015 [MANDATORY]**: Instrument type, asset class and GICS are separate classifications.
 
**RULE-016 [MANDATORY]**: GICS is used for applicable sector/industry grouping.
 
**RULE-017 [MANDATORY]**: Historical security master/prices required by reports are retained after a position is sold.
 
**RULE-018 [MANDATORY]**: System User and Household Member are separate concepts.
 
**RULE-019 [MANDATORY]**: Tax functionality is completely out of scope.
 
**RULE-020 [MANDATORY]**: Container type never constrains which account types may exist inside it.
 
**RULE-021 [MANDATORY]**: An account belongs to exactly one container; a container may be empty; a container roll-up may be negative.
 
**RULE-022 [MANDATORY]**: The same account type may occur several times within one container.
 
**RULE-023 [MANDATORY]**: Account type is immutable after creation.
 
**RULE-024 [MANDATORY]**: The transaction ledger is append-only; corrections are compensating entries.
 
**RULE-025 [MANDATORY]**: A provider-reported snapshot and a transaction are different record types, and neither is derived from the other.
 
**RULE-026 [MANDATORY]**: Stored prices are never retro-adjusted; adjusted series are derived from separately stored corporate actions.
 
**RULE-027 [MANDATORY]**: A fund maps to asset classes as a weighted set, never as a single class.
 
**RULE-028 [MANDATORY]**: Estimated, stale, carried-forward and unreconciled values are always visibly marked.
 
**RULE-029 [MANDATORY]**: Pension holdings participate in consolidated allocation on the same basis as any other holding.
 
**RULE-030 [MANDATORY]**: TWR requires a daily valuation series; the series is part of the core model, not of the reporting layer.
 
**RULE-031 [MANDATORY]**: A user override of any classification or value is never overwritten by an automated refresh.
 
**RULE-032 [MANDATORY]**: Licensed third-party reference data is never embedded in the distributed application or a public repository.
 
# 36. Conceptual Domain Model
 
SystemUser
+-- Role / Permissions
+-- Language
+-- ReportingCurrency
+-- optional link -> HouseholdMember
 
Household
+-- HouseholdMember / Ownership (share %, effective-dated)
+-- FinancialInstitution [0..n]            <<container>>
|   +-- ContainerCurrency
|   +-- Account [0..n]                     <<abstract>>
|       +-- AccountType (immutable)
|       +-- Capabilities (holds_positions, has_transactions,
|       |                 has_statement_cycle, has_amortisation,
|       |                 has_contribution_limit, is_discretionary,
|       |                 manual_valuation)
|       +-- Nature (asset | liability, derived from type)
|       +-- NativeCurrency
|       +-- Transaction [0..n]             (append-only)
|       +-- Position [0..n] -> Security
|       +-- Balance / Valuation
|       +-- Snapshot [0..n]                (provider-reported state)
|       +-- ReconciliationResult [0..n]
|       +-- DailyValuation [0..n]          (prerequisite for TWR)
+-- Asset / Liability
+-- Budget / Category / Rule
+-- Goal
 
Account specialisation (disjoint, total, one level deep):
  Cash | Savings | Securities/Depot | ManagedMandate | Pension |
  VestedBenefits | CreditCard | Mortgage | Loan | Crypto | CustomAsset
 
Security
+-- Issuer
+-- Identifiers (ISIN, Valor, WKN, ...)
+-- InstrumentType / CFI
+-- AssetClassWeight [1..n]                (weighted, not single-valued)
+-- GICS (8-digit + structure version)
+-- SNB Classification
+-- Listing [0..n]
|   +-- Exchange / MIC / Ticker / TradingCurrency
|   +-- TradingCalendar
|   +-- Price [0..n]                       (raw, immutable)
+-- CorporateAction [0..n]
+-- SuccessorLink
+-- MasterDataProvenance (per field)
 
FXRate
+-- BaseCurrency
+-- QuoteCurrency
+-- Date
+-- Rate
+-- Source
 
**Model notes.**
 
- Account specialisation is **disjoint** (exactly one subtype), **total** (no bare account) and **one level deep**. Variation within a subtype is expressed through capabilities, not through further sub-subtypes (FR-ACC-011).
 
- Every account type exposes the same valuation interface, so consolidation never branches on type (FR-ACC-012).
 
- Snapshot, DailyValuation and ReconciliationResult are the three entities most commonly omitted from designs of this kind, and their absence is the root cause of the accuracy problems visible in competing products.
 
# 37. MVP Baseline
 
The MVP shall establish a correct, extensible financial foundation. Priority areas:
 
- User administration, authentication baseline, user language and user reporting currency
 
- Household/member and ownership model
 
- Financial Institution/Container model including container currency and Institution Summary
 
- Account model for all required account types, with the capability model
 
- Unified transaction ledger, **template-driven multi-bank CSV import framework**, provenance, duplicate detection and rollback
 
- **Snapshots and the reconciliation engine, including manual snapshot entry and opening balances**
 
- Cash and credit-card categorization including MCC/source-classification separation
 
- Transfer, savings, investment and credit-card settlement rules
 
- Budgeting, cash-flow and net-worth reporting
 
- Security Master, listings, classifications and portfolio-driven lifecycle
 
- Historical prices and FX rates, **including backfill and the daily valuation series**
 
- Portfolio holdings, asset allocation, TWR, MWR and benchmark comparison
 
- Consolidated View in the user’s reporting currency
 
- English/German localization
 
- Privacy and security baseline, backup and restore *(user-facing export removed — OOS-008)*
 
- **Golden-dataset regression suite covering the cases in section 47**
 
- **Deletion, void and lifecycle-state rules (section 52); optimistic locking (section 53); reference-bundle import (section 54)**
 
**Sequencing note.** Items 6, 11 and 16 are the additions to the baseline MVP, and they are deliberately placed in the MVP rather than deferred. Reconciliation and the daily valuation series cannot be retrofitted without full historical recomputation, and the regression suite is what makes PR-010 enforceable rather than aspirational.
 
# 38. Post-MVP / Future Capabilities
 
- Institution-specific PDF imports
 
- Read-only Swiss/German bank and broker connectors
 
- Automatic recurring-transaction detection
 
- Upcoming bill forecasts
 
- Sankey cash-flow visualization
 
- ETF/fund look-through and direct/indirect GICS exposure
 
- Dividend calendar and forecasts
 
- Advanced financial-goal and retirement scenarios
 
- **User-facing data export in structured formats, with round-trip fidelity into a clean installation**
 
- Currency attribution reporting
 
- COICOP mapping and national spending benchmarks
 
- Self-hosted synchronization
 
- Optional managed/SaaS synchronization and backups
 
- Optional AI explanations operating on deterministic financial results
 
- French and Italian localization
 
Future capabilities shall not change the permanent exclusions for tax functionality, professional financial advice, payment execution, trade execution, private-equity valuation or public social networking.
 
