# 40. User Story Definition Standard
 
Each development-ready user story should include:
 
- Story ID and parent epic
 
- Actor/persona
 
- Business objective/rationale
 
- User story statement
 
- Preconditions
 
- Acceptance criteria (preferably Given / When / Then)
 
- Applicable business rules
 
- Data requirements and validation
 
- Error/edge cases
 
- Authorization/privacy requirements
 
- Dependencies
 
- Priority
 
- Definition of Done
 
- **Data-quality behaviour**: what the story does when the underlying data is incomplete, stale or unreconciled — required by PR-010 and PR-011, and the case most often missed in acceptance criteria
 
# 41. Inputs Required by the Software Architect
 
- Approved domain model and entity relationships
 
- Detailed business rules and calculation methodologies
 
- Data lifecycle/history/provenance requirements
 
- User/role/permission model
 
- Deployment topology behaviour and the online-only client model (sections 48, 30)
 
- Import and external connector contracts
 
- **Import template definition format** — the declarative schema in which per-institution CSV templates are expressed (FR-IMP-020/021)
 
- Security Master and price-data provider requirements
 
- GICS licensing approach and exact SNB taxonomy
 
- **External data entitlement and credential model for a locally installed application (NFR-LIC-002)**
 
- **Daily valuation series storage and recomputation strategy** (OPEN-017)
 
- **Volumetrics** (NFR-OPS-007) — outstanding
 
- **Confirmed household sharing model** (OPEN-021) — outstanding
 
- **Lifecycle state machine definitions** (FR-LIF-010)
 
- **Reference bundle format, schema and signing approach** (section 54)
 
- Performance, reliability, backup and privacy requirements
 
- Development-ready user stories and acceptance criteria
 
The technology baseline in section 48.3 fixes client framework, API framework, transport, authentication mechanism and database. All other technology choices — hosting, job scheduling, caching, observability, build and release tooling — remain the architect’s decision.
 
# 42. Open Decisions / Detailed Analysis Items
 
**OPEN-001 — CLOSED**: Browser plus native iOS and Android, via React Native + Expo (48.3).
 
**OPEN-002 — CLOSED**: PostgreSQL, server-side (48.3). Encryption-at-rest mechanism remains a deployment choice.
 
**OPEN-003 [TBD]**: Open-source license
 
**OPEN-004 [TBD]**: Swiss and German bank/broker *automated connector* strategy. Note this is separate from CSV import: the CSV route is template-driven and its institution list is a deferred delivery input (28.1.1), not an open decision.
 
**OPEN-005 [TBD]**: Market-data, master-data and FX providers
 
**OPEN-006 [TBD]**: Licensed GICS data source and redistribution/storage rights
 
**OPEN-007 [TBD]**: Exact SNB classification taxonomy and source
 
**OPEN-008 [TBD]**: Formal cost-basis/accounting method for portfolio history, excluding tax requirements
 
**OPEN-009 [TBD]**: Corporate-action and adjusted-price methodology
 
**OPEN-010 [TBD]**: Future multi-user household permission semantics
 
**OPEN-011 — CLOSED**: Self-hosting is a first-class topology from the outset (48.1). Client-server synchronisation is not required, as the client is online-only (NFR-CON-001).
 
**OPEN-012 [TBD]**: Do assets held with no provider (real estate, vehicles, private loans) attach to a default *Personal Assets* container, or may an account exist with no container? The baseline structure diagram implies the latter; rule C2 assumes the former. A privately held mortgage is the awkward case — it has a lender, which is arguably an institution. *Recommendation: default container, for tree uniformity.*
 
**OPEN-013 — CLOSED**: Resolved per topology (48.2). Hosted service uses the operator’s pooled licence; self-hosted uses the deploying user’s own entitlement or manual prices.
 
**OPEN-014 — CLOSED**: One deployment, one database, concurrent independent sessions with household-scoped isolation (48.4, 49).
 
**OPEN-015 [TBD]**: Is the Institution Summary displayable only in the container currency, or also switchable to the user reporting currency? The former is simpler and matches FR-INS-SUM-001; the latter is what many users will expect when comparing institutions.
 
**OPEN-016 [TBD]**: Which market leads — CH or DE? They require different first connectors, different institution catalogues and different launch messaging. Building both fully in parallel roughly doubles the critical path. DE is faster to reach because PSD2 connectivity is purchasable; CH is more defensible because bLink access and bank relationships are hard to replicate.
 
**OPEN-017 [TBD]**: Is the daily valuation series stored or recomputed on demand? Storage costs space and requires invalidation on backdated edits; recomputation costs time on every report. The choice affects the local performance targets in section 34.
 
**OPEN-018 [TBD]**: How is differentiation demonstrated without a tax export? The remaining advantages (section 1.2) are architectural and slower to show than a tax report would have been. Needs a positioning answer before any launch communication.
 
**OPEN-019 [TBD]**: Constituent-level look-through data source for funds. This is a separate licensed dataset from GICS itself, and without it sector analysis is unavailable for the majority of a typical portfolio (FR-GICS-005).
 
**OPEN-020 [TBD]**: Are discretionary mandates (VIAC, frankly, True Wealth) and Pillar 2 / vested-benefit accounts confirmed in MVP scope, or deferred? Both are proposed in sections 10.2 and 10.3 as SHOULD.
 
**OPEN-021 [TBD]**: Household sharing model — confirm the provisional resolution in FR-TEN-008. Strict isolation with explicit, revocable, granular grants, reconciling the “no user sees another user’s finances” requirement with household budgets and FR-HOU-004 privacy levels.
 
**OPEN-022 [TBD]**: Registration model for the hosted service — self-service signup, invite-only, or admin-provisioned. Section 8 currently describes admin provisioning, which suits self-hosting but not a public service.
 
**OPEN-023 [TBD]**: Web client approach — Expo web via react-native-web, or a separate React web client sharing a TypeScript API and domain layer. Decide after the transaction-list spike (RISK-010).
 
**OPEN-024 [TBD]**: Over-the-air update policy, specifically whether changes affecting displayed financial figures may bypass store review (RISK-011).
 
**OPEN-025 [TBD]**: Is MFA mandatory, optional, or configurable per deployment (FR-AUT-008)?
 
**OPEN-026 [TBD]**: Supported client-version window and forced-upgrade policy (FR-API-004).
 
**OPEN-027 [TBD]**: Hosting jurisdiction — Switzerland, EU/Germany, or separate deployments per region (NFR-DPR-001).
 
**OPEN-028 [TBD]**: Retention periods for financial records, audit logs and backups (NFR-DPR-005).
 
**OPEN-029 [TBD]**: Commercial model. Per-tenant hosting and pooled market-data licensing now carry recurring cost, so this is no longer deferrable.
 
**OPEN-030 [TBD]**: Volumetrics per NFR-OPS-007 — required before the schema and the section 34 targets can be validated.
 
**OPEN-032 [TBD]**: Soft-delete retention window before purge (FR-LIF-003). *Proposed: 30 days.*
 
**OPEN-033 [TBD]**: Reference-data staleness warning thresholds (FR-REF-008). *Proposed: warn at 6 months, prominently at 12.*
 
**OPEN-034 [TBD]**: Who curates and publishes reference bundles, where they are obtained, and at what cadence (FR-REF-012). Required before launch, since manual import is now the only update channel.
 
**OPEN-035 [TBD]**: Should one person be able to belong to two households — an adult managing their own and a parent’s finances? Currently unsupported (55.2).
 
**OPEN-031 [TBD]**: When does user-facing export return? Three consequences follow from its deferral and should be accepted knowingly rather than discovered later: no supported topology migration (NFR-DEP-006); no route for a user or adviser to derive tax figures externally, now that tax reporting is also out of scope (OOS-004); and no user-side recovery path if a hosted account is closed or the service is discontinued. None is a defect — export is a real cost and deferring it is a reasonable MVP decision — but each is a commitment made by omission.
 
# 43. Representative Acceptance Scenario
 
A representative household shall be able to model the following structure and receive correct consolidated results:
 
User A — Reporting Currency: CHF
 
Household
|
+-- PostFinance (Container Currency: CHF)
|   +-- Cash Account CHF
|   +-- Savings Account CHF
|   +-- Depot
|   +-- Credit Card
|
+-- DKB (Container Currency: EUR)
|   +-- Girokonto EUR
|   +-- Tagesgeld EUR
|   +-- Depot
|
+-- Broker X (Container Currency: USD)
|   +-- Cash USD / EUR / CHF
|   +-- Depot with USD/EUR/CHF listings
|
+-- VIAC (Container Currency: CHF)
|   +-- Pillar 3a (holding fund positions)
|
+-- Personal Assets (Container Currency: CHF)
    +-- Real Estate
    +-- Mortgage
 
Consolidated View -> CHF
 
The system shall correctly provide:
 
- Institution summaries in CHF, EUR and USD respectively
 
- A global consolidated overview in CHF for User A
 
- Historically correct FX conversion
 
- Categorized cash-account and credit-card spending
 
- No double counting of card settlement or internal transfers
 
- Assets, liabilities and historical net worth
 
- Consolidated portfolio holdings across depots
 
- Security Master information and historical prices
 
- Asset-class and GICS allocation
 
- TWR and MWR
 
- English/German user experience according to user preference
 
**Additional acceptance conditions introduced by this edition:**
 
- The **VIAC Pillar 3a fund holdings appear in the consolidated asset allocation and GICS analysis**, not only as a pension balance (FR-ALL-008)
 
- The same ETF held in both the DKB depot and the Broker X depot, under two listings in two currencies, consolidates into **one security exposure** with the correct total quantity (FR-DEP-003, FR-LST-004)
 
- Entering the PostFinance balance from a paper statement produces a **reconciliation result** identifying any difference against the ledger (FR-REC-002, FR-REC-006)
 
- The mortgage payment splits into interest and principal, so the savings rate is not understated (FR-CF-006)
 
- The Broker X USD position’s return is decomposable into local return and CHF/USD effect (FR-PERF-017)
 
- Where the server cannot reach its market-data providers, all of the above remain available from stored values, with prices marked stale (NFR-CON-003). Note that the client itself is online-only (NFR-CON-001): loss of client connectivity makes the application unavailable, and this is intended behaviour, not a defect.
 
# 44. Final Product Definition
 
TrackMyWealth is a bilingual, privacy-first personal and household financial management application for Switzerland and Germany, delivered as browser and native mobile clients against a REST API, and deployable either on infrastructure the household controls or as a hosted service. It models financial institutions as currency-aware containers containing different account types; categorizes bank and credit-card transactions for household reporting; centralizes portfolio security master data and historical prices; and consolidates assets, liabilities, cash flow and investment analytics into one user-defined reporting currency.
 
The system is designed to support both understandable household finance and advanced private-investor analysis while remaining under the user’s control. It provides information and analysis only: no payment execution, trade execution, professional financial advice, tax functionality, private-equity valuation or public social-network features.
 
Its distinguishing commitment is correctness: figures are reconciled against what institutions actually report, estimates are always marked as such, and methodology is documented and testable.
 
# 45. Risk Register
 
| ID | Risk | Impact | Mitigation |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| RISK-001 | **Wrong figures from corporate actions, missing cost basis or incomplete history.** Observed in competing products and the most common cause of user attrition in this category. | Very high — trust is not recoverable once lost | Reconciliation engine (12.1), golden dataset (47), visible flagging of estimates (PR-011), refusal to compute where data is insufficient (FR-PERF-018) |
| RISK-002 | **Connector breakage from institution-side changes.** A competitor experienced multi-week outages from one broker backend change. | High, recurring | Connector health and degradation (FR-IMP-014/015); manual and file import always available (FR-INS-010) |
| RISK-003 | **GICS licence terms incompatible with open-source self-hosting.** Redistributing licensed classification data in a downloadable deployment is unlikely to be permitted. | High — affects a MUST requirement | Fallback taxonomy (FR-GICS-011), distribution boundary (NFR-GICS-002), resolve OPEN-006 and OPEN-013 together and early |
| RISK-004 | **Market-data cost or terms unworkable for a free local product.** Providers generally licence to an identified operator, not to anonymous installations. | High | Resolve OPEN-013; documented degraded manual-price mode (NFR-LIC-004) |
| RISK-005 | **Swiss connectivity has no purchasable route.** Unlike PSD2 in Germany, Swiss access is contractual via bLink and bank agreements. | High | Treat as a long-lead item; do not place Swiss automated connectivity on the MVP critical path |
| RISK-006 | **Scope breadth.** The specification covers what four separate products do today. | High | Enforce the MVP cut (37); treat correctness rather than feature count as the differentiator |
| RISK-007 | **Post-launch data-model migration.** A competitor’s cash-account restructuring visibly moved user balances. | Medium–high | Cash, snapshots, ownership and weighted asset classes resolved in v1; NFR-REL-003 |
| RISK-008 | **Self-hosted durability.** A self-hosting household loses data to disk failure with no operator-side copy, and with export deferred has no secondary recovery route. | Medium | FR-BCK-004/005 backup prompting and verification; documented in self-hosting guidance |
| RISK-009 | **Single-maintainer sustainability** for an open-source financial product with recurring data-provider dependencies. | Medium | Address alongside OPEN-003 and OPEN-013 |
| RISK-012 | **Reference data goes stale under manual-only distribution.** Pension contribution limits above all: an outdated limit produces wrong goal tracking with no visible cause. | Medium | FR-REF-010/011 staleness warnings; effective-dated data marked estimated when no current entry exists |
| RISK-013 | **Household-splitting constraints erode before the feature arrives.** FR-HHL-001 to 005 are easy to violate accidentally once nobody remembers why they exist. | Medium | Automated check that no derived artefact embeds a household identifier; record the rationale at the ownership relation |
| RISK-014 | **Void-based correction is misread as a bug.** Users seeing a reversing entry alongside the original may report duplicated data. | Low | FR-LIF-003 presentation rules; voided items shown as voided, not duplicated |
 
# 46. Calculation Methodology Definitions
 
To be formally specified before implementation, satisfying NFR-CALC-003. Each definition shall state inputs, treatment of edge cases, rounding and worked examples.
 
| Metric | Definition to be fixed |
| --- | --- |
| **Net worth** | Sum of asset account values minus liability account values at a date, in the reporting currency, using valuation-date FX. Ownership share applied for person-scoped views. |
| **TWR** | Geometric linking of sub-period returns bounded by external cash flows into or out of the measured scope. Definition must state what counts as external at each scope level — a transfer between two of the user’s own depots is external to each depot but internal to the household. |
| **MWR** | Internal rate of return (XIRR) over dated external cash flows plus the closing value. Definition must state the convergence tolerance and the behaviour when no solution exists or several do. |
| **Modified Dietz** | Permitted approximation where intraday valuation is unavailable; the periods where it was applied must be recorded. |
| **Savings rate** | Definition must fix whether employer pension contributions, mortgage principal repayment and unrealised gains are included (FR-BUD-009). |
| **Cost basis** | FIFO default; treatment of fees; treatment of transferred-in positions with no reported basis; behaviour across corporate actions. |
| **Realised gain** | Proceeds minus cost basis of the matched lots, with fee treatment consistent with cost basis. |
| **FX conversion** | Which date’s rate applies to each figure class (FR-CUR-011); carry-forward rule for missing dates; direct-pair rule (FR-CUR-010). |
| **Position valuation** | Which listing’s price is used (FR-LST-004); carry-forward across non-trading days; staleness threshold. |
| **Currency attribution** | Decomposition of total return into local return and FX contribution, with the residual cross-term explicitly assigned. |
| **Reconciliation difference** | How a difference is computed, its tolerance, and when an account is considered reconciled. |
 
# 47. Verification Dataset
 
A fixed reference dataset with independently verified expected results, required by NFR-CALC-005. Minimum cases:
 
| # | Case | What it protects |
| --- | --- | --- |
| V-01 | Forward stock split | Quantity and historical price adjustment; unadjusted prices preserved |
| V-02 | Reverse split producing a fractional residue | Rounding policy; cash-in-lieu handling |
| V-03 | Merger, shares exchanged for shares | Successor linkage; continuity of position history |
| V-04 | Spin-off with cost-basis apportionment | Basis allocation method |
| V-05 | ISIN change with no economic event | That the position does not appear as sold and rebought |
| V-06 | In-kind transfer between custodians, no cost basis reported | FR-REC-008; estimated-basis flagging |
| V-07 | Partial sale under FIFO with three acquisition lots | Lot matching and realised gain |
| V-08 | USD position held with a CHF reporting currency, over a period of FX movement | Currency attribution; conversion date convention |
| V-09 | Dividend with withholding tax deducted | Gross versus net; cash balance correctness |
| V-10 | Large mid-period deposit followed by a market fall | Divergence of TWR and MWR — the two figures must differ, in the expected direction |
| V-11 | Account whose imported history begins two years after opening | Opening balance reconstruction; refusal to report meaningless earlier returns |
| V-12 | Same ETF in two depots, two listings, two currencies | Cross-depot consolidation; listing selection |
| V-13 | Credit-card purchase in month 1 settled from the current account in month 2 | Single expense recognition; transaction-date attribution |
| V-14 | Mortgage payment covering interest and principal | Expense versus balance-sheet split; savings-rate effect |
| V-15 | Multi-asset ETF holding equities and bonds | Weighted asset-class decomposition, not single-class assignment |
| V-16 | Pillar 3a holding equity funds | Inclusion in consolidated allocation and concentration |
| V-17 | Price series with a market holiday and a trading suspension | Carry-forward versus gap; no interpolation |
| V-18 | Same import file applied twice | Idempotency; duplicate detection |
| V-19 | Two structurally different bank CSV formats — semicolon/1.234,56/DD.MM.YYYY versus comma/1'234.56/ISO date | Template abstraction genuinely handles format variance |
| V-20 | CSV with separate debit and credit columns and a trailing summary row | Sign convention and non-data row handling |
| V-21 | Institution changes its export format between two imports | Template versioning; historical reproducibility (FR-IMP-023) |
 
Each case shall carry its expected output as a fixture, verified independently of the implementation.
 
# 48. Deployment Topologies, Technology Baseline and Tenancy
 
## 48.1 One architecture, two topologies
 
TrackMyWealth is delivered as a client application and a server API. The client never accesses the database directly. Consequently the same build serves both deployment topologies, and the difference between them is configuration and operational responsibility, not architecture.
 
|  | **Self-hosted** | **Hosted service** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Who runs the API and database | The user or household | The service operator |
| Typical tenants per deployment | One household | Many households |
| Registration | Bootstrap administrator at first run, then admin-provisioned users | Per business model (OPEN-022) |
| Market-data entitlement | Supplied by the deploying user, or manual prices | Operator’s licence, proxied |
| Licensed classification data (GICS) | Not distributed; fallback taxonomy or the user’s own entitlement | Operator’s licence |
| Backup | The deploying user | The operator, to section 51 targets |
| Data protection controller | The deploying user, for their own household | The operator |
 
**NFR-DEP-001 — Single codebase [MUST]**: Both topologies shall run the identical application, API and database schema. There shall be no self-hosted-only or hosted-only code path in the domain, calculation or authorization layers.
 
**NFR-DEP-002 — Differences are configuration [MUST]**: Topology differences shall be expressed as configuration — registration mode, data-provider credentials, backup scheduling, retention — never as compile-time variants.
 
**NFR-DEP-003 — Client is topology-agnostic [MUST]**: The client shall be pointed at an API base URL and shall behave identically regardless of where that API runs. The mobile applications shall permit configuring the server endpoint.
 
**NFR-DEP-004 — No trusted-deployment shortcut [MUST]**: Authorization, tenant isolation and audit shall be enforced identically in a single-household self-hosted deployment. A deployment believed to have one tenant is not permitted to relax any control; families self-host, and the multi-user requirement applies there too.
 
**NFR-DEP-005 — Self-hosted deployment is a first-class deliverable [MUST]**: Container images and a documented, reproducible deployment procedure including database migration shall be part of every release, not an afterthought.
 
**NFR-DEP-006 — Migration between topologies [COULD]**: Moving a household between topologies was previously to be achieved through export and import. With export deferred (OOS-008), no supported migration path exists in the MVP other than a full database transfer between deployments, which is only viable where the source deployment holds a single household. This is a consequence of the export decision and is recorded rather than solved. See OPEN-031.
 
## 48.2 Data entitlement per topology
 
This closes OPEN-013, which was unresolvable under the previous local-first assumption.
 
**NFR-LIC-001** (defined in section 30.1) continues to apply unchanged: API keys shall never be embedded in a distributed client or a public repository.
 
**NFR-LIC-005 — Server holds credentials [MUST]**: All external data provider credentials shall be held server-side and configured per deployment. Clients shall never hold a provider credential.
 
**NFR-LIC-006 — Hosted entitlement is shared and pooled [MUST]**: In the hosted service the security master, price series and FX series are shared across all tenants. Ten tenants holding the same ETF consume one price series and one provider request, not ten. This is the principal control on market-data cost.
 
**NFR-LIC-007 — Shared reference data is not tenant data [MUST]**: Shared master data carries no tenant identity and shall never be inferable back to which tenant caused it to be fetched.
 
**NFR-LIC-008 — Self-hosted degradation is documented [MUST]**: A self-hosted deployment without provider entitlement shall operate on manual prices (NFR-CON-004) and the internal fallback classification taxonomy (FR-GICS-011). This configuration shall be documented and supported, not presented as broken.
 
## 48.3 Technology baseline
 
The baseline recorded here supersedes the technology-neutral stance previously expressed in section 41 for these specific choices. Everything else remains the architect’s decision.
 
| Layer | Decision |
| --- | --- |
| Client | React Native + Expo, targeting iOS, Android and web |
| API | Spring Boot, REST, JWT authentication |
| Database | PostgreSQL |
 
**NFR-TEC-001 — Decimal types [MUST]**: Monetary and quantity values shall use PostgreSQL NUMERIC and Java BigDecimal end to end. Binary floating point shall not appear in any monetary path, including intermediate calculation and JSON serialisation.
 
**NFR-TEC-002 — Temporal types [MUST]**: Instants shall be stored as timestamptz; trade, booking and value dates as date. Mixing the two is a recurring source of off-by-one errors in performance calculation.
 
**NFR-TEC-003 — Time-series storage [MUST]**: Price, FX and daily-valuation tables are volume-dominant and shall be partitioned by time range.
 
**NFR-TEC-004 — Migrations are versioned and forward-only [MUST]**: Schema migrations shall be version-controlled, applied automatically on deployment, and never destructive to user-visible balances without in-application explanation (NFR-REL-003).
 
**RISK-010 — Data-dense web UI on react-native-web**: The product is substantially tables and charts — transaction lists with sorting, filtering, multi-column layout, keyboard navigation and CSV download. These are the weakest area of react-native-web. *Mitigation: run a spike on the transaction list screen before committing to Expo for web; the fallback is a separate React web client sharing a TypeScript API and domain layer.* See OPEN-023.
 
**RISK-011 — Over-the-air updates to calculation logic**: Expo OTA can deliver client changes bypassing store review. For a financial application, changes affecting displayed figures require a deliberate policy. See OPEN-024.
 
## 48.4 Tenancy and data isolation
 
The controlling requirement: **an authenticated user shall see only data they are entitled to, and by default that is only their own.**
 
**FR-TEN-001 — Tenant is the household [MUST]**: The isolation boundary shall be the household. Every financial record shall be attributable to exactly one household.
 
**FR-TEN-002 — Deny by default [MUST]**: Access shall be denied unless an explicit grant exists. There shall be no implicit visibility derived from being in the same household, the same deployment, or the same database.
 
**FR-TEN-003 — Isolation enforced at the data layer [MUST]**: Tenant isolation shall be enforced in PostgreSQL — row-level security or an equivalent mechanism that cannot be bypassed by a query written in the application. Service-layer filtering alone is insufficient: a single forgotten predicate in one query is the entire failure.
 
**FR-TEN-004 — Object-level authorization on every access [MUST]**: Every read and write shall verify that the authenticated principal is entitled to that specific object, not merely to the endpoint. Broken object-level authorization — retrieving another user’s record by changing an identifier — is the most common serious API vulnerability and is precisely the failure mode this requirement exists to prevent.
 
**FR-TEN-005 — Non-enumerable identifiers [MUST]**: Externally visible identifiers shall be non-sequential and non-guessable.
 
**FR-TEN-006 — Consistent denial [MUST]**: An unauthorized request for an existing object and a request for a non-existent object shall be indistinguishable in response and in timing, so that identifiers cannot be probed.
 
**FR-TEN-007 — Administrative rights are not financial rights [MUST]**: A System Administrator can manage users, roles and system configuration. This shall confer **no** access to any household’s financial data. The two permission domains are separate (FR-USR-010), and this separation shall hold in a self-hosted deployment where the administrator is also a household member — their financial access derives from their household membership alone.
 
**FR-TEN-008 — Sharing is explicit and granular [MUST]**: Where household members share financial visibility, it shall arise from an explicit, revocable grant at account or scope level, with the levels of FR-HOU-004 — no access, balance only, read, edit, full. Shared constructs such as household budgets shall be modelled as objects owned by the household with their own grants, not as an implied merge of members’ private data. *Provisional resolution of the contradiction noted in review; confirm as B5.*
 
**FR-TEN-009 — Grant changes are immediate and audited [MUST]**: Revoking a grant shall take effect on the next request, not on token expiry (see FR-AUT-006).
 
**FR-TEN-010 — Cross-tenant test suite [MUST]**: An automated test suite shall attempt cross-tenant access against every endpoint and every object type, and shall run in continuous integration. Isolation is asserted by tests, not by inspection.
 
**FR-TEN-011 — Aggregates respect scope [MUST]**: Every aggregate, chart and export shall be computed strictly within the requesting principal’s authorized scope, with the scope visible (FR-CON-006). A shared account in a joint household shall be counted exactly once (FR-HOU-005).
 
# 49. Authentication, Sessions and Authorization
 
**FR-AUT-001 — Concurrent independent sessions [MUST]**: Multiple users shall be able to authenticate and operate simultaneously against the same deployment, with fully independent sessions and no shared state between them.
 
**FR-AUT-002 — Multiple sessions per user [MUST]**: One user may hold concurrent sessions on several devices — phone, tablet, browser. Sessions shall be individually listable and revocable by that user.
 
**FR-AUT-003 — Short-lived access tokens [MUST]**: JWT access tokens shall be short-lived. A long-lived access token is not revocable and is unacceptable for financial data.
 
**FR-AUT-004 — Refresh tokens are opaque, stored and rotated [MUST]**: Refresh tokens shall be server-side records rather than self-contained claims, shall rotate on each use, and reuse of a consumed refresh token shall invalidate the entire token family and be treated as a suspected theft event.
 
**FR-AUT-005 — Revocation is real [MUST]**: Logout, password change, session revocation, grant revocation, role change and user deactivation shall take effect within the access-token lifetime at worst, and immediately for security-significant events. The design shall state explicitly how this is achieved — a token version or session identifier checked per request is the usual mechanism. *JWT statelessness is not an acceptable justification for delayed revocation.*
 
**FR-AUT-006 — Token storage per platform [MUST]**: Web shall use an httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie for the refresh credential and shall not place tokens in localStorage. Mobile shall use iOS Keychain and Android Keystore. These are different threat models and shall be specified separately.
 
**FR-AUT-007 — Password policy and hashing [MUST]**: A modern memory-hard password hash with per-user salt. Minimum-length-led policy rather than composition rules. Breached-password checking may be offered but shall be optional and privacy-preserving, and shall never be a hard dependency in a self-hosted deployment (NFR-PRIV-002).
 
**FR-AUT-008 — MFA [MUST]**: TOTP multi-factor authentication shall be supported. Whether it is mandatory is a policy decision per deployment (OPEN-025).
 
**FR-AUT-009 — Passkeys [SHOULD]**: WebAuthn/passkey authentication should be supported on all three client platforms.
 
**FR-AUT-010 — Rate limiting and lockout [MUST]**: Authentication endpoints shall be rate-limited per account and per source, with exponential backoff, without enabling denial of service against a legitimate user.
 
**FR-AUT-011 — Biometric re-entry is a local unlock only [MUST]**: Mobile biometric unlock shall gate access to a stored refresh credential. It is not an authentication factor against the server and shall not be presented as one.
 
**FR-AUT-012 — Re-authentication for sensitive operations [SHOULD]**: Changing credentials, granting financial access to another member and deleting an account shall require recent authentication.
 
**FR-AUT-013 — Authentication audit [MUST]**: Successful and failed authentications, token-family invalidations, grant changes and role changes shall be recorded with timestamp, principal and source. Financial data content shall not be written to these logs.
 
**FR-AUT-014 — Optional OIDC [COULD]**: Self-hosted deployments may benefit from delegating authentication to an existing identity provider.
 
# 50. API Contract and Client Applications
 
## 50.1 API
 
**FR-API-001 — Specification is the contract [MUST]**: An OpenAPI specification shall be generated from, and validated against, the implementation, and shall be a release deliverable.
 
**FR-API-002 — Explicit versioning [MUST]**: The API shall be versioned. Breaking changes require a new version; additive changes shall not break existing clients.
 
**FR-API-003 — Version skew is permanent [MUST]**: Clients ship through app stores and update on their own schedule, so old clients calling a newer API is the normal condition, not an exception. The API shall support a declared window of prior client versions, and the client shall degrade gracefully — an unknown field is ignored, an unavailable capability is hidden rather than crashing.
 
**FR-API-004 — Minimum supported client [MUST]**: A minimum supported client version shall be enforced, with a clear upgrade message rather than an opaque failure. See OPEN-026 for the support window.
 
**FR-API-005 — Consistent error envelope [MUST]**: A single machine-readable error format shall carry a stable code, a human-readable message, and a correlation identifier. Errors shall never leak internal detail or the existence of other tenants’ data.
 
**FR-API-006 — Idempotent writes [MUST]**: State-changing operations shall accept an idempotency key so that a retry after a network failure cannot create a duplicate transaction. This is a direct consequence of the online-only, mobile-network reality of NFR-CON-002.
 
**FR-API-007 — Pagination and filtering [MUST]**: Collection endpoints shall be paginated with stable ordering. Transaction queries shall support the filters of FR-TRX-005 server-side; filtering shall never require the client to download the full set.
 
**FR-API-008 — Long-running work is asynchronous [MUST]**: Imports, historical recomputation, price backfill and reconciliation runs shall be submitted as jobs returning a status resource, not held open on a request.
 
**FR-API-009 — Rate limiting [MUST]**: Per-principal rate limits with informative headers.
 
**FR-API-010 — Payload discipline [SHOULD]**: Responses shall be shaped for the screens that consume them. Aggregation belongs on the server; the client shall not compute portfolio figures locally, both for payload size and to guarantee one authoritative implementation of every calculation (NFR-CALC-002).
 
**FR-API-011 — Monetary values in transport [MUST]**: Monetary amounts shall be transported as strings with an explicit currency, never as JSON numbers, to avoid float coercion in the client.
 
## 50.2 Clients
 
**FR-CLI-001 — Three targets, one codebase [MUST]**: iOS, Android and web from the shared React Native + Expo codebase, subject to the web caveat in RISK-010.
 
**FR-CLI-002 — No client-side source of truth [MUST]**: The client holds no authoritative data and performs no financial calculation. Cached responses are presentation state only.
 
**FR-CLI-003 — Cache is cleared on logout and on user switch [MUST]**: All cached financial data, images and derived view state shall be discarded on logout, on session revocation and on switching user. No residue of one user’s data shall be observable to another user of the same device.
 
**FR-CLI-004 — Cached data is encrypted at rest on device [MUST]**: Any financial data persisted on the device shall be encrypted using platform storage facilities.
 
**FR-CLI-005 — Screen protection [SHOULD]**: Suppress sensitive content in the application switcher snapshot; consider blocking screenshots on balance screens.
 
**FR-CLI-006 — Session timeout and re-lock [MUST]**: Configurable inactivity timeout requiring biometric or credential re-entry.
 
**FR-CLI-007 — Server endpoint configuration [MUST]**: The client shall permit configuring the API endpoint so that a self-hosted deployment can be used from the published applications (NFR-DEP-003).
 
**FR-CLI-008 — Certificate and transport security [MUST]**: TLS enforced; consider certificate pinning for the hosted service, but pinning shall not prevent connection to a self-hosted deployment with an operator-supplied certificate.
 
**FR-CLI-009 — Accessibility [MUST]**: WCAG 2.2 AA for the web client and the platform accessibility equivalents on mobile. *Absent from the baseline; added here as a decision to confirm.*
 
**FR-CLI-010 — No third-party analytics on financial screens [MUST]**: No financial data, and no data permitting its inference, shall be transmitted to third-party analytics or crash-reporting services. Crash reporting, if used, shall be opt-in and scrubbed, and disabled by default in self-hosted deployments.
 
# 51. Operations, Background Processing and Data Protection
 
## 51.1 Background processing
 
**FR-JOB-001 — Scheduled work is server-side [MUST]**: Price and FX refresh, daily valuation series maintenance, recurring-rule application and scheduled reconciliation run on the server, independently of any client being connected.
 
**FR-JOB-002 — Jobs are idempotent and resumable [MUST]**: A job interrupted or re-run shall not duplicate data or corrupt derived state.
 
**FR-JOB-003 — Tenant fairness [MUST]**: In a multi-tenant deployment, one tenant’s large import or recomputation shall not degrade interactive response for others.
 
**FR-JOB-004 — Background work never blocks interaction [MUST]**: Recomputation shall not lock interactive reads; the user shall see a clear in-progress state rather than an unresponsive screen.
 
**FR-JOB-005 — Provider request efficiency [MUST]**: Price and FX retrieval shall be batched and deduplicated across tenants (NFR-LIC-006) and shall respect provider rate limits with backoff.
 
**FR-JOB-006 — Backdated edits trigger targeted recomputation [MUST]**: Editing or importing a transaction dated in the past shall invalidate and rebuild only the affected derived data from that date forward. See OPEN-017 on whether the daily valuation series is stored or computed on demand.
 
**FR-JOB-007 — Job observability [MUST]**: Job status, last successful run and failures shall be visible to a deployment administrator, and failures affecting a household’s figures shall be visible to that household as a data-quality indicator (FR-CON-007).
 
## 51.2 Hosted service operations
 
These apply to the hosted topology. A self-hosted operator is responsible for the equivalent and shall be given guidance rather than requirements.
 
**NFR-OPS-001 — Backup objectives [MUST]**: Recovery point and recovery time objectives shall be defined and met. *Proposed: RPO 24 hours, RTO 8 hours; confirm as C15.*
 
**NFR-OPS-002 — Restores are tested [MUST]**: Restore from backup shall be exercised on a defined schedule. An untested backup is not a backup.
 
**NFR-OPS-003 — Backups are encrypted and access-controlled [MUST]**.
 
**NFR-OPS-004 — Availability target [SHOULD]**: Availability target to be defined; degraded market data shall not render the service unavailable (PR-012).
 
**NFR-OPS-005 — Monitoring excludes financial content [MUST]**: Logs, traces and metrics shall not contain financial data or personal identifiers beyond an opaque principal reference.
 
**NFR-OPS-006 — Environment separation [MUST]**: Production data shall never be copied into development or test environments. Test data shall be synthetic — the golden dataset of section 47 serves this purpose.
 
**NFR-OPS-007 — Volumetrics [MUST — INPUT REQUIRED]**: Capacity and performance design requires the figures below. They are not yet supplied and are required before the schema and the targets in section 34 can be validated.
 
| Dimension | Required figure |
| --- | --- |
| Accounts per household | *to be supplied* |
| Transactions per household per year | *to be supplied* |
| Securities held per household | *to be supplied* |
| Years of history retained | *to be supplied* |
| Distinct securities across all tenants | *to be supplied* |
| Tenants at launch / at 3 years | *to be supplied* |
| Peak concurrent sessions | *to be supplied* |
 
**NFR-OPS-008 — Revised performance targets [MUST]**: The targets in section 34 were written for a local dataset and shall be restated as API server-side latency, payload size, and end-to-end time on a mobile network, once NFR-OPS-007 is supplied.
 
## 51.3 Data protection
 
Holding Swiss and German residents’ financial data on an operated service makes the operator a controller under the Swiss revised FADP and the EU GDPR. This obligation did not exist under the previous local-first assumption and is new to this edition.
 
**NFR-DPR-001 — Declared jurisdiction and residency [MUST]**: The hosting jurisdiction shall be declared, and data shall not leave it without a documented lawful basis. See OPEN-027 on whether CH and EU require separate deployments.
 
**NFR-DPR-002 — Lawful basis and processing record [MUST]**: Documented for every category of processing, with a maintained subprocessor register covering market-data and classification providers.
 
**NFR-DPR-003 — Subject rights [MUST]**: Access, rectification, erasure and portability shall be operable within statutory deadlines. With user-facing export deferred, portability is satisfied by the operator-side procedure of FR-POR-001, which shall be documented and tested before the hosted service accepts its first external user.
 
**NFR-DPR-004 — Deletion is real and bounded [MUST]**: Household erasure shall remove tenant data within a stated period, including from backups within the published retention window. Erasure operates at household level, not selectively within history (FR-LIF-020). Shared reference data is not tenant data and is unaffected (NFR-LIC-007).
 
**NFR-DPR-005 — Retention policy [MUST]**: Retention periods for financial records, audit logs and backups shall be defined. See OPEN-028.
 
**NFR-DPR-006 — Breach response [MUST]**: A documented breach detection and notification process meeting both regimes’ deadlines.
 
**NFR-DPR-007 — Provider disclosure minimisation [MUST]**: Requests to external providers shall be aggregated across tenants so that a provider cannot attribute a security lookup to an individual household (NFR-SEC-006, NFR-LIC-007).
 
**NFR-DPR-008 — Self-hosted controller guidance [SHOULD]**: Documentation shall make clear that a self-hosting household is its own controller and that the project processes none of their data.
 
**NFR-DPR-009 — Independent security assessment [SHOULD]**: A penetration test covering authentication, authorization and tenant isolation before the hosted service opens to users outside the project.
 
# 52. Data Lifecycle, Deletion and Retention
 
## 52.1 Governing principle
 
**The only hard delete in the system is tenant erasure. Everything else is void or archive.**
 
This follows from three requirements already in force and is not an independent design preference. The ledger is append-only (FR-TRX-007); every derived figure must be reproducible from source records (PR-005, FR-DAT-008); and reconciliation compares ledger-derived state against provider snapshots (FR-REC-002). A hard-deleted transaction breaks all three at once — historical TWR changes silently, and a reconciliation that passed last month fails this month with no visible cause. An audit trail records that a deletion happened but does not prevent the derived data from becoming wrong.
 
## 52.2 Deletion rules by entity
 
**FR-LIF-001 — Deletion matrix [MUST]**
 
| Entity | Rule | Hard delete permitted |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Transaction | Void via a reversing entry; original retained | Never |
| Position | Derived; never directly deleted | Never |
| Snapshot | Superseded by a later snapshot; retained | Never |
| Import batch | Rollback as hard delete **only while unmodified** (52.3) | Conditionally |
| Account | Archive once it holds any transaction, position or snapshot | Only if empty |
| Financial institution | Delete only when it contains no accounts | Only if empty |
| Category | Deactivate; historical assignments preserved | Only if never used |
| Categorization rule | Delete freely; not historical data | Yes |
| Budget / Goal | Archive; historical periods preserved | Only if never active |
| Household member | Deactivate if they own or co-own anything | Only if they own nothing |
| System user | Disable; linked member data unaffected (FR-USR-011) | Only if never linked |
| Security master record | Retained while referenced (FR-SMD-005) | Only if unreferenced |
| Household (tenant) | **Hard delete — the erasure path** | Yes |
 
**FR-LIF-002 — Void semantics [MUST]**: Voiding a transaction shall create a reversing entry dated to the void, referencing the original, and shall mark the original with voided_at, voided_by and a reason. Both records remain in the ledger. Derived data shall be recalculated from the affected date forward (FR-JOB-006).
 
### Refinement: transaction removal depends on provenance
 
A flat void-everything rule is correct for imported data but poor usability for manual entry. Requiring a compensating entry to correct a typo the user made thirty seconds ago pollutes the ledger without protecting anything. The permitted operation therefore depends on where the record came from and whether it has been reconciled.
 
**FR-LIF-002a — Removal tiers [MUST]**
 
| Tier | Applies to | Permitted operation | Why |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **T1 — Soft delete** | Manually entered, never reconciled, not referenced by a reconciliation resolution | Soft delete; hidden from all views; recoverable for the restore window (FR-LIF-006); purged thereafter | The user is the sole source of truth. There is no institutional record to preserve and no reconciliation assertion to disturb. |
| **T2 — Void only** | Imported from any source — file, document or connector | Void with a reason; remains visible in the provenance view; excluded from figures | The record is evidence of what the institution reported. Reconciliation compares against it (FR-REC-002), so removing it destroys the ability to explain a difference later. |
| **T3 — Void reopens reconciliation** | Any record included in a completed reconciliation, however it originated | Void with a reason; the affected reconciliation returns to OPEN and is flagged for review (FR-STA-003) | A reconciled figure was asserted correct. Changing its inputs invalidates that assertion and the user must be told. |
 
**FR-LIF-002b — Tier is determined by the system, not chosen [MUST]**: The applicable tier follows from the record’s provenance and reconciliation state. The user is not offered a choice between soft delete and void.
 
**FR-LIF-002c — Bulk removal only by batch [MUST]**: Imported records shall be removed in bulk only by rolling back their import batch (52.3), never by multi-select deletion.
 
**FR-LIF-003 — Void may be presented as deletion [SHOULD]**: The interface may label voiding as “delete”. The user need not understand the mechanism; the ledger must retain it. Where a voided item appears in a historical view it shall be shown as voided rather than silently omitted.
 
**FR-LIF-004 — Correction versus void [MUST]**: Editing non-financial attributes — description, category, notes — is a normal update. Editing any attribute affecting a financial figure — amount, currency, date, quantity, price, account — shall be recorded as a void plus a replacement entry, so the change is visible in history.
 
**FR-LIF-005 — Archive semantics [MUST]**: An archived account or member is excluded from current totals and from selection lists, but fully included in every historical figure covering a period in which it was active (FR-ACC-006).
 
**FR-LIF-006 — Restore window [MUST]**: Voided transactions and archived accounts shall be restorable through the interface for 30 days. Thereafter they remain in the data but are no longer user-restorable.
 
**FR-LIF-007 — Cascade is explicit and previewed [MUST]**: Any action affecting dependent records shall state what will be affected before confirmation — how many transactions, which positions, which budgets. There shall be no silent cascade.
 
**FR-LIF-008 — No orphans [MUST]**: Referential integrity shall be enforced at the data layer: no account without a container, no transaction without an account, no position without a security.
 
## 52.3 Import batch rollback
 
**FR-LIF-010 — Unmodified batch rollback [MUST]**: An import batch may be hard-deleted while none of its records has been modified — not edited, not manually categorised, not referenced by a reconciliation resolution, not voided. Rejecting an import nobody has worked on is a different act from editing history; treating it as a void would litter the ledger.
 
**FR-LIF-011 — Modified batch rollback [MUST]**: Once any record in a batch has been modified, rollback shall void the batch’s records rather than delete them, and shall report which records were retained and why.
 
**FR-LIF-012 — Rollback is atomic [MUST]**: Rollback shall fully succeed or fully fail (NFR-REL-001).
 
## 52.4 Retention and erasure
 
**FR-LIF-020 — Tenant erasure [MUST]**: Deleting a household shall remove all of its data — transactions, accounts, snapshots, derived data, grants, and audit entries containing personal data — within the period stated in NFR-DPR-004, including from backups within the published retention window.
 
**FR-LIF-021 — Shared reference data survives erasure [MUST]**: Security master records, price series and FX series carry no tenant identity and are unaffected (NFR-LIC-007).
 
**FR-LIF-022 — Erasure is the only selective-deletion answer [MUST]**: The system shall not offer deletion of individual historical records to satisfy an erasure request. Erasure operates at household granularity. This shall be stated in the privacy documentation, as it is a consequence of the append-only ledger.
 
**FR-LIF-023 — Erasure is confirmed and delayed [MUST]**: Tenant erasure shall require recent authentication (FR-AUT-012), explicit confirmation, and a cancellable grace period before execution.
 
# 53. Household Lifecycle
 
Confirmed direction: household splitting is **not an MVP feature**, but the model shall not foreclose it. Anticipating it now costs four constraints and no functionality. Retrofitting it later means rewriting ownership, grants and every historical aggregate.
 
## 53.1 Constraints enabling later splitting
 
**FR-HHL-001 — Ownership is an effective-dated association [MUST]**: Ownership shall be a dated relationship between household member and account, carrying a share where fractional — never a column on the account (FR-HOU-006).
 
**FR-HHL-002 — Household assignment is mutable [MUST]**: An account’s household assignment shall be changeable. No query, index, cache or derived artefact may assume it is fixed for the account’s lifetime.
 
**FR-HHL-003 — No denormalised household identifier in derived data [MUST]**: Derived artefacts shall not embed a household identifier in a way that cannot be rebuilt from source records (FR-DAT-008). Isolation predicates shall resolve household membership through the ownership relation rather than a copied key.
 
**FR-HHL-004 — Membership history is retained [MUST]**: Joining and leaving shall be recorded with effective dates rather than overwriting current membership.
 
**FR-HHL-005 — Grants are dated [MUST]**: Sharing grants (FR-TEN-008) shall carry effective dates, so a historical view can establish who had access to what and when.
 
## 53.2 Policy decisions recorded in advance
 
**FR-HHL-010 — History follows the account [MUST]**: Where an account moves to another household, its complete history moves with it, and the originating household’s historical reports change retroactively as a result. The alternative — history staying behind — leaves both parties permanently incomplete, which is worse. Recorded now so that no interim design assumes the opposite.
 
**FR-HHL-011 — Retroactive change is disclosed [MUST]**: Where a structural change causes historical figures to change, affected households shall be informed in-application (NFR-REL-003).
 
**FR-HHL-012 — Member deactivation retains history [MUST]**: A departing member is deactivated; their historical ownership and the transactions of accounts they owned remain with the household unless and until those accounts are moved.
 
**FR-HHL-013 — One household per person in MVP [MUST]**: Multi-household membership — someone managing their own and a relative’s finances — is out of MVP scope. The model shall not preclude it: the member-to-household relation shall be structurally many-to-many even while the application enforces one. See OPEN-032.
 
**FR-HHL-014 — Death and inheritance out of scope [MUST]**: No estate-transfer functionality. In practice handled by deactivating the member, and later by the splitting capability.
 
**FR-HHL-015 — Last member protection [MUST]**: A household shall always retain at least one active member with full access, by analogy with FR-USR-005.
 
# 54. Concurrency
 
Concurrent independent sessions (FR-AUT-001) combined with shared household objects (FR-BUD-003, FR-TEN-008) make simultaneous modification a normal occurrence, not an edge case.
 
**FR-CNC-001 — Optimistic locking on financial entities [MUST]**: Every mutable financial entity shall carry a version. A write shall fail if the version advanced since the client read it. Last-write-wins is not acceptable where two household members may edit a shared budget.
 
**FR-CNC-002 — Conflict behaviour [MUST]**: On version conflict the API shall return a distinct error (FR-API-005); the client shall inform the user, show current state, and require explicit re-attempt. No merge interface is required in the MVP.
 
**FR-CNC-003 — Bulk operations are transactional [MUST]**: Import commit, batch rollback, retroactive rule application and reconciliation resolution shall be atomic (NFR-REL-001).
 
**FR-CNC-004 — Recomputation does not block reads [MUST]**: A recomputation triggered by one user shall not block another’s reads in the same household. Readers may see pre-recomputation values provided the in-progress state is indicated (FR-JOB-004).
 
**FR-CNC-005 — Serialised derived-data writes per account [MUST]**: Two processes shall not write derived data for the same account simultaneously; rebuilds shall be serialised per account.
 
**FR-CNC-006 — Idempotency covers double submission [MUST]**: Idempotency keys (FR-API-006) shall protect against the same logical write arriving twice from a retry or a double tap, not only from network retries.
 
**FR-CNC-007 — Import concurrency [MUST]**: Two simultaneous imports into the same account shall be serialised or rejected with a clear reason. Interleaved duplicate detection across two in-flight batches need not work.
 
# 55. Reference Data Distribution
 
Confirmed direction: **manual file import only.** No update endpoint, no automatic retrieval, no phone-home. This fully honours NFR-PRIV-002 and removes an outbound dependency from the self-hosted topology.
 
## 55.1 What counts as reference data
 
Data that must be updatable independently of application logic: the institution catalogue (FR-INS-007), CSV import templates (FR-IMP-020), source-code-to-category mappings (FR-CAT-010), pension contribution limits and deadlines (FR-PEN-006), the GICS structure version (FR-GICS-007), and the fallback classification taxonomy (FR-GICS-011).
 
## 55.2 Distribution mechanism
 
**FR-REF-001 — Baseline ships with the release [MUST]**: Every release shall include a complete baseline reference-data set. A fresh installation shall never start with an empty institution catalogue or no import templates. *This resolves the ambiguity in the manual-import decision: the release is the initial channel; manual import is the update channel between releases.*
 
**FR-REF-002 — Manual import is the update channel [MUST]**: Between releases, reference data shall be updatable by importing a package file through the administration interface. There shall be no automatic retrieval.
 
**FR-REF-003 — No outbound dependency [MUST]**: The application shall make no network call for the purpose of obtaining reference data, in either topology.
 
**FR-REF-004 — Versioned package format [MUST]**: Packages shall carry a version, publication date, compatible application version range, and a content manifest.
 
**FR-REF-005 — Integrity verification [MUST]**: Packages shall be integrity-checked on import — signature or checksum. Reference data is administrator-imported and shapes financial categorisation; an unverified package is an injection route.
 
**FR-REF-006 — Validated and previewed [MUST]**: Import shall validate against the expected schema, show what will change, and be rejected in full if invalid. Partial application is not permitted.
 
**FR-REF-007 — Versioned in place [MUST]**: Importing a package shall not discard a prior version where historical reproducibility depends on it — notably GICS structure versions (FR-GICS-007) and effective-dated pension limits (FR-PEN-006).
 
**FR-REF-008 — Rollback [SHOULD]**: The previous reference-data version shall be restorable.
 
**FR-REF-009 — Never overwrites user data [MUST]**: A package shall never overwrite user-created institutions, user-defined templates, user category customisations or user overrides (FR-CAT-014, FR-SMD-013).
 
## 55.3 Staleness
 
Manual-only distribution means reference data can silently age. For date-sensitive values this produces wrong figures with no visible cause, which conflicts with PR-011.
 
**FR-REF-010 — Version is visible [MUST]**: Current reference-data version and publication date shall be shown in the administration interface.
 
**FR-REF-011 — Date-sensitive staleness warning [MUST]**: Where reference data carries effective-dated values — pension contribution limits above all — and no entry covers the current period, the application shall warn and shall mark derived figures as estimated. It shall not silently apply a superseded limit.
 
**FR-REF-012 — Publication [SHOULD]**: Packages shall be published at a stable, documented location an administrator can check. Distribution is manual; discovery need not be.
 
# 56. Entity Lifecycle States
 
Formal state definitions for entities whose lifecycle is otherwise described only in prose. Transitions not listed are prohibited.
 
**FR-STA-001 — Account [MUST]**
 
| State | Meaning | Transitions to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ACTIVE | In use; in current totals | ARCHIVED |
| ARCHIVED | Closed; out of current totals, in history (FR-LIF-005) | ACTIVE, within the restore window |
| DELETED | Reachable from ACTIVE only if the account never held data | terminal |
 
**FR-STA-002 — Import batch [MUST]**
 
| State | Meaning | Transitions to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| UPLOADED | File received, not parsed | PARSED, FAILED |
| PARSED | Validated; preview available; nothing committed | COMMITTED, DISCARDED |
| COMMITTED | Records written to the ledger | ROLLED_BACK, VOIDED |
| ROLLED_BACK | Hard-deleted; only from an unmodified batch (FR-LIF-010) | terminal |
| VOIDED | Records voided rather than deleted (FR-LIF-011) | terminal |
| DISCARDED | Preview abandoned before commit | terminal |
| FAILED | Unparseable (FR-IMP-027) | terminal |
 
**FR-STA-003 — Reconciliation difference [MUST]**
 
| State | Meaning | Transitions to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OPEN | Detected, unresolved | RESOLVED, ACCEPTED, DISMISSED, SUPERSEDED |
| RESOLVED | Cause corrected; ledger agrees | terminal |
| ACCEPTED | Provider figure accepted via a visible adjusting entry (FR-REC-004) | terminal |
| DISMISSED | Consciously ignored, reason recorded | OPEN |
| SUPERSEDED | A newer snapshot replaced the basis of comparison | terminal |
 
**FR-STA-004 — Security master record [MUST]**
 
| State | Meaning | Transitions to |
| --- | --- | --- |
| ACTIVE | Referenced by a current position or watchlist; refreshed (FR-SMD-006) | INACTIVE |
| INACTIVE | Referenced only by history; retained, not refreshed (FR-PRC-004) | ACTIVE |
| DELISTED | No longer traded; last price frozen | SUPERSEDED |
| SUPERSEDED | Replaced after a corporate action; successor linked (FR-SMD-010) | terminal |
 
**FR-STA-005 — Background job [MUST]**: QUEUED → RUNNING → SUCCEEDED, FAILED or CANCELLED, with RETRYING between RUNNING and a terminal state. Jobs shall be idempotent under retry (FR-JOB-002).
 
**FR-STA-006 — Session [MUST]**: ACTIVE → EXPIRED or REVOKED. Revocation is immediate (FR-AUT-005).
 
**FR-STA-007 — Household member [MUST]**: ACTIVE → INACTIVE, reversible. DELETED reachable only from a member who has never owned anything (FR-LIF-001).
 
**FR-STA-008 — Transitions are audited [MUST]**: Every transition of an account, import batch, reconciliation difference or member shall be recorded with timestamp and actor.
 
# 57. Financial Change Audit
 
FR-DAT-005 covers administrative actions and FR-AUT-013 covers authentication. Neither records who changed financial data, which matters in a shared household.
 
**FR-AUD-001 — Financial change log [MUST]**: Creation, modification, void and restore of any financial record shall be recorded with timestamp, actor, entity, and the before and after values of changed fields.
 
**FR-AUD-002 — Visible to the household [SHOULD]**: A member with appropriate access shall be able to see the change history of a record they can view.
 
**FR-AUD-003 — Audit is append-only [MUST]**: Audit records shall not be modifiable or deletable through the application, and shall be removed only by tenant erasure (FR-LIF-020).
 
**FR-AUD-004 — Reads are not audited [MUST]**: Read access is not audited by default, to bound volume. See OPEN-033 if a stricter posture is required.
 
# 58. Glossary
 
| Term | Meaning in this specification |
| --- | --- |
| **Financial Institution / Container** | Grouping level for accounts held with one provider. Not itself an account. May be a bank, broker, pension provider, card issuer, asset manager, or the default Personal Assets container. |
| **Account** | A single financial product within a container. Abstract; always of exactly one concrete type. |
| **Capability** | Declared property determining what an account can do, independent of its type. |
| **Transaction** | A dated financial event. |
| **Snapshot** | A provider-reported observation of state at a point in time — balance and/or holdings. Not derivable from transactions. |
| **Position** | A holding of a security within an account. |
| **Security Master** | Centralised reference data for a security, shared by all positions referencing it. |
| **Listing** | A security as traded on one exchange, with its own ticker, trading currency and price series. |
| **Reconciliation** | Comparison of ledger-derived state against a snapshot, and the identification of any difference. |
| **Daily valuation series** | Per-account value at each date; prerequisite for TWR. |
| **TWR** | Time-weighted return — measures the investment strategy, removing the effect of contribution timing. |
| **MWR** | Money-weighted return — measures the user’s actual experience, including contribution timing. |
| **Reporting currency** | The currency of a user’s global consolidated view. |
| **Container currency** | The currency of one institution’s summary. |
| **Estimated value** | Any figure derived from incomplete data — always visibly marked. |